The Wrong Queue
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid2375315 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 
The difficulty with queues, Neville Wren had always felt, was not that they were slow.
\par Slowness, while regrettable, was at least honest. A slow queue made no promises except eventual disappointment, and there was a kind of dignity in that. No, the real difficulty with queues was that they encouraged hope in manageable portions, which was fa
r more dangerous. You advanced by inches, watched your fellow citizens undergo minor spiritual decay in front of you, and came at last to the desk only to discover that your existence required Counter Four, a corridor, and a form obtainable exclusively fr
om the place you had just left.
\par This was the ordinary version.
\par The queue to which Neville had now been summoned was not the ordinary version.
\par He and Mrs Elspeth Chain followed the signs down two flights of stairs, along a beige corridor devoted entirely to lowering expectations, and through a pair of glass doors bearing the legend:
\par QUEUE MANAGEMENT\line For the Avoidance of Unauthorised Resolution
\par \'93That,\'94 said Mrs Chain, reading it once and rejecting it absolutely, \'93is a revolting sentence.\'94
\par \'93Yes,\'94 said Neville. \'93It\rquote s new.\'94
\par Inside, the room was larger than it had any right to be.
\par Queue Management occupied what must once have been an atrium, though one that had since been persuaded to regret its openness. A high ceiling hung above ranks of chrome stanchions and retractable belts arranged into lanes of such subtle cruelty that one s
uspected professional input. At the far end stood six service desks beneath softly glowing signs:
\par INITIAL DETERMINATIONS\line REVISIONAL DETERMINATIONS\line POST-DETERMINATE APPEALS\line PRE-RESOLVED EXCEPTIONS\line INTENT ALIGNMENT\line GENERAL ENQUIRIES / NO
\par Between the doors and the desks stood perhaps fifty people in a queue which immediately, and with appalling confidence, divided into six.
\par Neville stopped.
\par Mrs Chain stopped beside him.
\par \'93I see,\'94 she said after a moment, \'93that this is idiotic.\'94
\par Neville stared ahead. \'93Yes.\'94
\par \'93How does one join it?\'94
\par \'93I\rquote m not yet sure it can be joined in the classical sense.\'94
\par At the entrance stood a free-standing pillar dispensing numbered tickets. This, in any civilized institution, would have been reassuring. Neville pressed the button.
\par The machine emitted a mild chime and produced three tickets.
\par A17.\line A17 (provisional).\line A17/b.
\par Neville looked down at them in silence.
\par Mrs Chain took one, adjusted her spectacles, and read it.
\par \'93What,\'94 she asked, \'93is a provisional seventeen?\'94
\par \'93I would assume,\'94 said Neville, \'93a seventeen that has not committed.\'94
\par \'93And the b?\'94
\par \'93I refuse to speculate.\'94
\par Near the pillar a young man in a reflective waistcoat was attempting to provide guidance to the public in the bright, exhausted tone of someone whose training had been both inadequate and recent.
\par \'93If you are here regarding an unresolved outcome,\'94 he was saying, \'93please proceed to the queue most closely aligned with your present state. If uncertain, join the uncertainty lane until a more definite uncertainty becomes available.\'94
\par A woman clutching a pram said, \'93Which one is the uncertainty lane?\'94
\par The young man looked around helplessly. \'93At the moment, madam, all of them.\'94
\par Mrs Chain closed her eyes briefly. \'93Oh, very good. They\rquote ve hired a poet.\'94
\par Neville examined the room.
\par The six lanes were not stable. That was the first problem. The second was that they were not, in any useful sense, separate. The queue seemed to divide and rejoin itself according to principles more suggestive of philosophy than architecture. A man in a p
instripe suit stood simultaneously in two adjacent lanes by means of a posture so strained that it deserved a pension. An elderly couple had somehow entered the Revisional Determinations line and emerged, several yards farther on, in General Enquiries / N
o. A student with a rucksack advanced almost to the front of Initial Determinations only to be gently redirected sideways by an apologetic barrier.
\par Neville watched for perhaps ten seconds and had the distinct feeling that the room was selecting answers based on the questions people brought into it.
\par He disliked rooms that had opinions.
\par \'93Mr Wren?\'94
\par He turned. A woman was approaching from Desk Three with a file tucked under one arm and the expression of a person who had once been patient by temperament and had since overcome it. She was in her forties, perhaps, dressed in dark grey with a badge clipp
ed so exactly to her lapel that it appeared pinned there by moral force alone.
\par BEATRIX SLOAN\line Internal Audit
\par She looked first at Neville, then at Mrs Chain, then at the three contradictory queue tickets in Neville\rquote s hand, and seemed not at all surprised.
\par \'93You\rquote re the Chain case,\'94 she said.
\par Neville inclined his head. \'93Neville Wren. Transit Reconciliation.\'94
\par \'93I know.\'94 She held out a hand to Mrs Chain. \'93Sloan. Internal Audit.\'94
\par Mrs Chain shook it. \'93Are you in charge of this?\'94
\par \'93No,\'94 said Beatrix. \'93Otherwise it would be less decorative.\'94
\par She took one of Neville\rquote s tickets and read it without approval. \'93They\rquote ve started branching admissions.\'94
\par \'93What does that mean?\'94 asked Neville.
\par \'93It means,\'94 said Beatrix, \'93
that rather than deciding what category a problem belongs in, the department now permits the problem to be processed under several mutually incompatible interpretations until one of them becomes administratively cheaper.\'94
\par Mrs Chain considered this. \'93That sounds immoral.\'94
\par Beatrix gave a tiny shrug. \'93Yes, but efficiently so, which is the current preference.\'94
\par She turned and set off toward the lanes without waiting to see if they followed, which they did, because some people generate obedience merely by walking as though corridors owe them truth.
\par At the mouth of the queue Beatrix stopped and pointed.
\par \'93Look carefully,\'94 she said.
\par Neville did.
\par To an untrained eye, the movement of the lanes might have appeared merely disorderly. But once one attended to the signs, a pattern emerged. Citizens approaching with misplaced luggage tended to be pulled toward General Enquiries / No. Those with contradi
ctory documents drifted toward Revisional Determinations. People with the stunned, inward-looking expression of persons whose day had become ontological were drawn, slowly but unmistakably, toward Intent Alignment.
\par \'93Is it sorting by complaint type?\'94 Neville asked.
\par \'93Not exactly,\'94 said Beatrix. \'93It\rquote s sorting by interpretive frame.\'94
\par Mrs Chain gave her a sharp look. \'93In English.\'94
\par Beatrix obliged at once. \'93The room decides what sort of problem you are having, and then forces you to become that sort of problem.\'94
\par Neville felt a small, unpleasant quickening in the back of his mind. \'93Based on what?\'94
\par \'93On what is observed first,\'94 said Beatrix.
\par There are sentences which, to a former physicist trapped in public administration, land with the emotional force of a brick through stained glass.
\par Neville looked again at the room.
\par A man holding a damp briefcase was being asked, at Desk One, whether his issue concerned delay, duplication, or symbolic displacement. Each answer altered the forms placed before him. A woman near Desk Five had apparently described her missing husband as 
both late and increasingly hypothetical, and was now being redirected toward Post-Determinate Appeals with an orange folder marked DOMESTIC AMBIGUITY.
\par Neville said quietly, \'93It\rquote s choosing a basis.\'94
\par Beatrix looked at him. \'93I beg your pardon?\'94
\par \'93The system,\'94 he said, more quickly now. \'93It isn\rquote t merely receiving complaints. It\rquote s measuring them according to whichever question it asks first. Delay, identity, route, intent. The categories aren\rquote 
t neutral. They determine the answer.\'94
\par Mrs Chain folded her arms. \'93So if I say I took the wrong bus, that\rquote s one problem. And if I say I arrived before deciding, that\rquote s another.\'94
\par \'93Exactly,\'94 said Neville.
\par \'93But they are the same thing.\'94
\par \'93Yes.\'94
\par \'93That\rquote s monstrous.\'94
\par \'93Yes,\'94 said Beatrix. \'93But very modern.\'94
\par Neville took out his pencil and, balancing the green notebook against one hand, wrote:
\par System selects }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 |question\\rangle}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315  before }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 |answer\\rangle}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 
\par Then below it:
\par No single queue. Multiple measurement frames.
\par Mrs Chain peered at the page. \'93That\rquote s not going to improve matters, is it.\'94
\par \'93No,\'94 said Neville. \'93But it may help identify the species of disaster.\'94
\par They entered the lane marked INTENT ALIGNMENT.
\par At once the carpet changed colour from apologetic blue to a slightly sterner shade of blue, as if to indicate that uncertainty was tolerated here only under supervision. The barriers to either side gave a faint electric hum. Ahead of them stood five citiz
ens in various states of administrative fatigue.
\par The first was a man in cycling clothes trying to explain that his office had received him before he had left his house, though only emotionally. The second was a schoolteacher insisting that her afternoon had been reassigned to Thursday without consultati
on. The third, a nervous teenager with an oblong parcel, kept muttering that he had only meant to visit his grandmother \'93in principle.\'94
\par As the line advanced, Neville noticed something even less encouraging.
\par The questions changed people.
\par A clerk at the front desk, hidden until now by a partition, was speaking in a soothing voice to the cyclist.
\par \'93Did you intend,\'94 she asked, \'93to go to work?\'94
\par \'93Yes, obviously.\'94
\par \'93Fully?\'94
\par The cyclist blinked. \'93What?\'94
\par \'93Would you say your intention was complete, partial, or symbolic?\'94
\par \'93Complete, I suppose.\'94
\par The clerk ticked a box.
\par At once the screen beside her updated:
\par COMMUTER MISSEQUENCING\line SUBTYPE: PRE-COMMITTED ARRIVAL
\par The cyclist looked down at the label that had printed for him, and in that instant his whole complaint seemed to shrink. He began repeating the clerk\rquote 
s terminology back to her, as though his own experience had already been translated into something smaller and easier to store.
\par Neville felt cold.
\par The teacher stepped up next. Her account of a missing afternoon was reframed, within seconds, as TEMPORAL MISALLOCATION WITH EDUCATIONAL IMPACT. By the time she left the desk, she seemed almost persuaded that the matter concerned staffing.
\par Mrs Chain said under her breath, \'93They file you until you agree.\'94
\par \'93Yes,\'94 said Neville.
\par Beatrix, ahead of them, nodded once. \'93That\rquote s the model.\'94
\par Then it was Mrs Chain\rquote s turn.
\par The clerk looked up with the professionally laundered smile of a person trained never to acknowledge originality in suffering.
\par \'93Good morning. Nature of unresolved intent discrepancy?\'94
\par Mrs Chain set her handbag down on the counter with deliberate care. \'93I went to visit my sister in Whitbury-on-Sea.\'94
\par \'93Certainly.\'94
\par \'93I had not entirely decided to go.\'94
\par \'93Yes.\'94
\par \'93And when I arrived, the town seemed to be missing part of its sea.\'94
\par The clerk\rquote s fingers paused above the keyboard.
\par \'93Was the destination emotionally authorized prior to embarkation?\'94
\par Mrs Chain stared at her. \'93It was what?\'94
\par \'93Emotionally authorized.\'94
\par \'93No,\'94 said Mrs Chain. \'93It was my sister.\'94
\par The clerk blinked. \'93Would you classify your journey as voluntary, reluctant, habitual, or familial?\'94
\par Mrs Chain drew herself up. \'93I would classify it as none of your business.\'94
\par Neville almost admired the speed with which Beatrix stepped in.
\par \'93Internal Audit,\'94 she said, placing her badge on the counter. \'93This case is under review.\'94
\par The clerk\rquote s face changed by perhaps half a millimetre, which in administrative environments counts as a passionate emotional event.
\par \'93Of course,\'94 she said. \'93Then I\rquote ll need Form IA-9 for diverted interpretive handling.\'94
\par \'93You will not,\'94 said Beatrix. \'93You will give me the current classification tree and the routing logic for this lane.\'94
\par \'93That material is not available at desk level.\'94
\par \'93Then fetch someone to whom availability has happened.\'94
\par The clerk hesitated.
\par There are many people in bureaucracy who wield power, but very few who wield certainty. Beatrix Sloan wielded certainty in a way that made lesser officials briefly consider confession.
\par The clerk rose and vanished through a side door.
\par Mrs Chain turned. \'93I like her.\'94
\par \'93I noticed,\'94 said Neville.
\par \'93Does she ever smile?\'94
\par \'93Possibly in private,\'94 he said.
\par \'93Wasteful.\'94
\par While they waited, Neville examined the counter terminal. On the edge of the screen, where some programmer had assumed no one with curiosity would ever be employed, ran a diagnostic panel:
\par PRIMARY MEASUREMENT FRAME: INTENT\line SECONDARY FRAME: ROUTE\line TERTIARY FRAME: CIVIC STABILITY\line AUTO-COLLAPSE ENABLED: YES
\par Neville\rquote s mouth went dry.
\par He copied it into the notebook.
\par Auto-collapse.
\par That was not a phrase one used by accident.
\par He drew a line beneath it and wrote:
\par Ask different first question }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 \\rightarrow}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315  obtain different world.
\par A quiet voice at his elbow said, \'93That\rquote s true more often than people care to admit.\'94
\par Neville turned.
\par The speaker was the thin young man he had noticed only in passing when they entered: dark coat, narrow face, stack of sealed envelopes under one arm, the general air of someone who belonged everywhere on a provisional basis. He had somehow arrived beside 
them without ever seeming to cross the intervening space.
\par \'93I\rquote m sorry?\'94 said Neville.
\par The young man nodded toward the notebook. \'93Different question, different world. Sensible. Bit gloomy, but sensible.\'94
\par Mrs Chain eyed him. \'93Were you in this queue?\'94
\par \'93Briefly,\'94 he said.
\par \'93And now?\'94
\par \'93I\rquote ve improved my position by not belonging to it.\'94
\par Beatrix looked over. \'93Who are you.\'94
\par \'93Jago Flint.\'94
\par He produced an identification card so quickly and casually that Neville entirely missed where it had been kept.
\par Interdepartmental Courier Service\line Restricted / Floating Clearance
\par \'93Floating clearance?\'94 Neville asked.
\par Jago smiled. \'93Means I\rquote m authorized where things aren\rquote t fully settled.\'94
\par \'93That can\rquote t be an actual credential,\'94 said Neville.
\par \'93Mr Wren,\'94 said Jago pleasantly, \'93neither, at present, can your sea.\'94
\par Before Neville could reply, the side door reopened and the clerk returned with three forms, a thin man in a mustard tie, and an atmosphere of immediate uselessness.
\par \'93This is Deputy Lane Supervisor Pritchard,\'94 she said.
\par Pritchard had the stooped, hesitant manner of a man who had been promoted specifically because he would not resist language. He gave them all a smile of deep institutional sadness.
\par \'93What seems to be the difficulty?\'94
\par Beatrix handed him Mrs Chain\rquote s printout, Neville\rquote s contradictory tickets, and the audit badge in one economical movement.
\par \'93The difficulty,\'94 she said, \'93is that Queue Management is collapsing cases into interpretive states based on intake phrasing.\'94
\par Pritchard glanced at the papers. \'93Ah.\'94
\par \'93Do you deny it?\'94
\par \'93No,\'94 he said. \'93That is the intended service enhancement.\'94
\par Neville spoke before he could help himself. \'93Service enhancement?\'94
\par \'93Yes. Citizens were becoming distressed by prolonged ambiguity. The new system ensures each event is processed under a stable descriptive regime at the earliest convenience.\'94
