\par Mrs Chain set down the paper and picked up the umbrella from the stand.
\par Neville, recognising the posture of a woman about to improve technology by force, said, \'93Possibly not\emdash \'94
\par Too late.
\par She struck the mirror smartly across the middle.
\par The text vanished.
\par The mirror cracked in a neat diagonal line and, for the first time all day, something in the house seemed honestly itself.
\par Jago let out a breath that might almost have been admiration. \'93That,\'94 he said, \'93felt clinically significant.\'94
\par Beatrix bent to inspect the broken frame. Behind it, fixed to the plaster, was a small grey module no larger than a cigarette case, humming very softly.
\par Neville stared. \'93What is that.\'94
\par Beatrix prised it loose. On the back was stamped:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 CIVIC STABILITY SUBSTRATE \emdash  HOME EDITION}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 
\par Beneath it, in smaller type:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 Property of the Simplification Initiative}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2375315\charrsid2375315 
\par Neville took the device in both hands. It was warm.
\par Too warm.
\par \'93It\rquote s local,\'94 he said. \'93The revision isn\rquote t only happening in central records. They\rquote re installing reality correction hardware in people\rquote s houses.\'94
\par Mrs Chain said, with terrible calm, \'93Remove it.\'94
\par Neville looked uncertainly at Beatrix.
\par Beatrix said, \'93Do it.\'94
\par Jago stepped back into the doorway. \'93Before you do, a practical note.\'94
\par Neville glanced up. \'93What practical note.\'94
\par \'93If this thing is part of a wider correction network, unplugging it may tell someone that Unit 7B has become ideologically restless.\'94
\par Mrs Chain tightened her grip on the umbrella. \'93Excellent.\'94
\par Neville turned the device over. On one edge was a recessed catch.
\par He pressed it.
\par The module clicked.
\par For one instant nothing happened.
\par Then everything in the room became uncertain.
\par The clock vanished, reappeared, and turned into a photograph of a man with spectacles. The daffodils in the hall browned into geraniums, then back again, then both at once in a manner Neville found deeply rude. The carpet pattern shifted underfoot from st
ripes to faded blue swirls to bare boards and back. Somewhere upstairs, a door opened and shut in several incompatible styles.
\par Mrs Chain grasped the banister.
\par Neville felt the air thicken around him, not physically but informationally, as though the house had ceased to know which version of itself it had most recently been ordered to become.
\par He opened the notebook with one hand and wrote almost blindly:
\par System disturbed.\line Underlying states still present.\line False certainty was holding them down.
\par Beside him Beatrix said, \'93Wren.\'94
\par He looked up.
\par At the far end of the hall, where the front door should have shown a small rectangle of afternoon light, there was now a man in a dark coat standing just inside the threshold.
\par No one had heard the door open.
\par No one had heard him enter.
\par He held a slim case in one hand and wore the faint, polished smile of someone whose job consisted entirely of describing intolerable things as procedural necessities.
\par He inclined his head.
\par \'93Good afternoon,\'94 he said. \'93Continuity Review.\'94
\par Mrs Chain lifted the umbrella a little higher.
\par The man\rquote s smile remained in place with professional courage.
\par \'93I\rquote m afraid,\'94 he said, \'93someone appears to have interrupted an authorized domestic convergence.\'94
\par Neville looked down at the humming module in his hands, at the cracked mirror, at the photograph that was still trying intermittently to become a clock, and then back at the man in the doorway.
\par For the first time that day, the problem seemed almost refreshingly simple.
\par \'93Oh,\'94 he said. \'93Was that yours.\'94
