\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14043652 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14043652\charrsid14043652 
There are several ways in which a stranger may stand in one's hallway and become immediately unwelcome.
\par He may be damp. He may be cheerful. He may carry a clipboard with the sort of ease that suggests long moral decline. Or, as in the present case, he may incline his head by half an inch and say "Continuity Review" as though this were the sort of phrase a d
ecent person ought to find reassuring.
\par The man in Mrs Chain's doorway managed all of these conditions except dampness, which only served to make the rest of him more offensive.
\par He was in a dark coat of almost expensive sobriety, with a narrow tie and a face that looked as though it had once tried charm and settled, after careful reflection, on procedure. In his right hand he carried a slim case of brushed grey metal. In his left
 he held a card badge displaying a silver spiral and the words:
\par MINISTRY OF CIVIC STABILITY\line CONTINUITY REVIEW\line Field Resolution
\par Mrs Elspeth Chain tightened her grip on the umbrella.
\par "I'm afraid," said the man pleasantly, "someone appears to have interrupted an authorised domestic convergence."
\par Neville Wren looked down at the warm grey module in his hands, then back at the visitor. "Yes," he said. "That does seem, in hindsight, to have occurred."
\par The man stepped one shoe inside the threshold, then paused as if waiting for the house to endorse him. It did not, exactly, but a lamp in the sitting room brightened by a degree that felt sycophantic.
\par "My name is Alistair Venn," he said. "Senior Review Officer, Continuity. If you'd all remain calm, this can be resolved locally."
\par Mrs Chain gave a short laugh of such pure disbelief that even the daffodils in the hall seemed to reconsider themselves.
\par "Mr Venn," she said, "you have entered my house during a metaphysical burglary and used the word locally."
\par "I do understand this is a distressing transition."
\par "It is not a transition," said Mrs Chain. "It is vandalism with signage."
\par Beatrix Sloan had already moved to one side of the hall, where she stood with her arms folded and the air of an auditor preparing to improve a man's afternoon beyond recovery.
\par "Show me your authority," she said.
\par Venn smiled. "Internal Audit."
\par "Yes."
\par "How comforting."
\par "No," said Beatrix, "specific."
\par Venn set the slim case on the hall table\emdash teal, Neville noticed with renewed disgust\emdash and removed a folded document stamped with too many approvals to be morally sound.
\par "Phase One continuity authority," he said. "Regional domestic stabilisation. Sub-clause twelve permits local correction where unresolved identity adhesion threatens public confidence."
\par Mrs Chain pointed the umbrella at him. "Public confidence in what."
\par Venn's smile remained in place with admirable institutional training. "In dwellings, among other things."
\par Jago Flint, who had taken up a position against the wall in the vague manner of a man unwilling to commit to being fully in one room, said, "That's not an answer, is it."
\par "It is the one currently issued," said Venn.
\par Neville, still holding the detached module, felt the house shifting minutely around him. The cracked mirror in the hall was attempting to decide whether it had always been cracked, and the clock in the sitting room had, for the moment, become a photograph
 again. One sensed a large number of small possibilities pressing upward through a badly fitted administrative lid.
\par Venn noticed the module in Neville's hand.
\par "If you'd return that to me," he said, "I can re-establish coherence."
\par Neville did not move. "What exactly does it do."
\par "It supports stable domestic identity."
\par "By replacing people's teacups," said Mrs Chain, "and misfiling their dead."
\par Venn gave her a patient look. "By reducing low-value divergence."
\par There are phrases which, once spoken aloud, remove any residual doubt as to whether a person deserves tea.
\par Beatrix said, "Define low-value."
\par "Features whose emotional significance exceeds their civic necessity."
\par Mrs Chain stared at him. "You ghastly man."
\par Venn ignored this with professional ease. "Now then. If we proceed promptly, most residual variance can be contained."
\par He opened the slim case.
\par Inside lay a small handheld scanner, three blank forms, a silver pen, and a set of coloured tabs arranged with the sort of order one associates with people who have mistaken neatness for ethics.
\par Neville felt a tightening at the back of his mind.
\par "What do the tabs do," he asked.
\par "Nothing dramatic," said Venn. "They classify continuity pressure."
\par "That is a dramatic sentence," said Jago.
\par Venn selected a pale yellow tab and affixed it to the top of one form.
\par "Site unstable," he murmured. "Occupant resistant. Decorative conflict moderate."
\par Mrs Chain made a sound like a kettle about to begin legal proceedings.
\par Then Venn raised the scanner and pointed it at the mantelpiece.
\par A soft tone sounded.
\par The photograph of the spectacled man flickered and became, for half a second, the clock again.
\par Neville stepped forward. "Stop that."
\par Venn lowered the device. "Mr\emdash "
\par "Wren."
\par "Mr Wren, your interference is understandable, but not helpful. The property is currently exhibiting unresolved domestic supernumeraries. Until I determine which elements belong to the authorised continuity path, the environment may display minor symbolic
 agitation."
\par "Symbolic agitation," said Beatrix. "Is that what you call deleting a woman's life."
\par "No," said Venn. "That would be an escalation."
\par The room went still.
\par Even Jago seemed briefly at a loss for an appropriate angle of insolence.
\par Neville looked at the scanner. "You're measuring the house object by object."
\par "Of course."
\par "And each reading selects a version."
\par Venn tilted his head. "That is a somewhat theatrical way of putting it."
\par "It is an accurate one."
\par "Accuracy," said Venn, "is not always the same thing as usefulness."
\par This was, Neville felt, the sort of remark that ought to disqualify a man from discussing reality.
\par Venn turned to Mrs Chain. "I'll need to confirm a few details to reduce remaining branch pressure."
\par "No," said Mrs Chain.
\par "I'm afraid yes."
\par "No," she repeated, with the sort of clarity one associates with church bells and artillery.
\par Venn consulted the form as if the form might lend him courage.
\par "Was your late husband," he asked, "habitually visible in the front room."
\par Mrs Chain blinked. "What."
\par "On display," Venn clarified. "Photographic representation, commemorative object, or emotional proxy."
\par Neville shut his eyes briefly.
\par Beatrix said, "You cannot possibly think that is an admissible question."
\par Venn sighed the sigh of a man burdened by other people's attachment to language. "We have found that unresolved bereavement often creates object density around obsolete personal states."
\par Mrs Chain advanced one step.
\par "My husband," she said, "was not an obsolete personal state."
\par Venn made a small note on the form.
\par "High memorial adhesion," he murmured.
\par Jago straightened from the wall. "I wouldn't write that down if I were you."
\par Venn, not yet appreciating the counsel, continued. "Now then. Sister present or absent in core domestic narrative?"
\par Mrs Chain simply looked at him.
\par Venn tried again. "Would you say your sister is structurally central to your self-model."
\par Mrs Chain turned to Neville. "Mr Wren."
\par "Yes."
\par "If I strike him, will the paperwork improve."
\par "No," said Neville honestly. "But I am beginning to see its appeal."
\par Venn raised the scanner again, this time toward the bookshelves.
\par The titles shimmered.
\par Pleasant Soups Through the Year became Regional Stews for Quiet Evenings, then Domestic Warmth: A Practical Guide, then settled once more on soup.
\par Neville stared.
\par The scanner was not detecting a stable reality. It was forcing a choice each time it asked.
\par Question. Narrowing. Selection.
\par He opened the green notebook and wrote quickly:
\par Repeated measurement reducing state space.
\par Not finding truth.\line Projecting onto permitted answers.
\par Underneath that he added:
\par The house is larger than the form.
\par He looked up and said, "You're not restoring anything. You're compressing it."
\par Venn glanced over with mild interest, as one might regard a spaniel that had unexpectedly solved an invoice.
\par "Compression," he said, "is an inelegant term."
\par "It's exact."
\par "It is practical."
