This chapter teaches state reduction by repeated measurement. Continuity Review does not discover one pre-existing truth; it repeatedly asks narrow questions and forces the house, objects, and memories into a smaller permitted set of answers.
\par The key lesson is that coarse, intrusive measurement can destroy structure. The Ministry's system treats rich human reality like compressible data, projecting it into an administratively acceptable subspace until only a thinner, duller version remains.

\par It also sets up a deeper idea for later chapters: suppressed alternatives are not always gone. Sometimes they are still there underneath, held down by constant observation, classification, and power.
