\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid473883 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid473883\charrsid473883 
By the time they reached the Regional Compression Annex, the day had acquired the exhausted brightness peculiar to national mistakes.
\par The building stood behind two fences, one camera mast, and a sign which read:
\par CIVIC INFRASTRUCTURE HARMONISATION SITE\line Authorised access only\line No public gravitation beyond this point
\par \'93There,\'94 said Jago Flint, slowing the car, \'93is a sentence nobody wrote with a clear conscience.\'94
\par The annex had once been a distribution hub, though years of government improvement had polished it into the sort of place that no longer admitted to having had a function. Its walls were pale, windowless, and faintly curved in a way that suggested either 
modern design or local damage to geometry. Several service roads led toward it and then, halfway there, seemed to lose confidence and become lay-bys.
\par Stephanie Fainrose observed all this without visible surprise.
\par \'93That\rquote s new,\'94 she said.
\par \'93The building?\'94 asked Neville.
\par \'93The curvature suppression.\'94
\par He looked again. Now that she had said it, the air above the concrete yard did seem oddly mannered, as if distances were being encouraged not to express themselves fully.
\par Beatrix Sloan had already stepped out of the car and was walking toward the gate with the clipped inevitability of an approaching audit.
\par \'93Do we have entry authority,\'94 said Neville.
\par \'93No,\'94 said Beatrix.
\par \'93Then what exactly is the plan.\'94
\par \'93I am going to ask in a tone that makes refusal sound career-limiting.\'94
\par Mrs Elspeth Chain emerged from the back seat with her umbrella and looked up at the sign.
\par \'93No public gravitation,\'94 she read. \'93They always phrase wickedness as if it were a leisure rule.\'94
\par The gate guard, whose relationship with sleep appeared to have become historical, took one look at Beatrix\rquote s identification, one look at Fainrose\rquote 
s face, and decided that regulations were excellent in principle but perhaps not in this specific hour.
\par They were admitted.
\par Inside, the annex was colder than the day required. Corridors extended in several directions under long white lights, all of them labelled with the sort of terminology institutions use when trying to make dangerous machinery sound recyclable. Signs pointe
d toward VECTOR SANITISATION, METRIC STORAGE, and ANCHOR MANAGEMENT. One arrow had been hastily covered over, but not so well that Neville could not still read GRAVITY WELL OBSERVATION beneath the tape.
\par \'93Subtle,\'94 said Jago.
\par \'93It used to be worse,\'94 said Fainrose.
\par \'93You\rquote ve been here.\'94
\par \'93Once. I resigned shortly afterwards.\'94
\par This seemed fair.
\par They passed through two security doors, a control station, and a room full of humming cabinets attended by three technicians who possessed the unmistakable air of people who had obeyed instructions for so long that reality itself now felt above their pay 
grade. On the far wall, a large display showed the same descending trace they had seen at Fainrose\rquote s house, only here it was surrounded by status lights, internal bulletins, and an amber warning banner that read:
\par ANCHOR THERMAL DRIFT WITHIN PROJECTED ENVELOPE\line No intervention required at this time
\par Fainrose stopped dead.
\par \'93Oh,\'94 she said softly. \'93Now I\rquote m angry.\'94
\par One of the technicians rose. He was young, eager, and had the brittle optimism of a man who believed complex systems were improved by badges.
\par \'93This area is restricted,\'94 he began.
\par Beatrix placed the printed diagnostic from Fainrose\rquote s bench on the console.
\par \'93No,\'94 she said, \'93this area is evidence.\'94
\par He looked down at the page, then up at Fainrose, and visibly regretted several earlier life choices.
\par \'93We\rquote ve been assured,\'94 he said, \'93that the anchor is shedding excess load in a controlled manner.\'94
\par Fainrose stared at him.
\par \'93That sentence,\'94 she said, \'93contains so little understanding I scarcely know where to begin.\'94
\par Neville moved closer to the master display. Several subsidiary windows were open around the main mass-loss curve. One showed municipal transport routes blinking in and out like doubtful constellations. Another listed compression burdens by district. A thi
rd, to his growing unease, contained a stream of domestic reconciliation events, each with timestamps and area codes.
\par Mrs Chain saw it too.
\par \'93My house,\'94 she said.
\par There it was. CHAIN, E.\line Residential convergence: incomplete.\line Residual identity conflict: elevated.\line Overflow channel: accepted.
\par Neville read the last line twice.
\par Overflow channel.
\par He turned to Fainrose. \'93They really are using the singularity as a sink.\'94
\par \'93Yes,\'94 she said. \'93For unresolved alternatives, unstable classifications, excess correlation, and anything else their system cannot afford to keep locally.\'94
\par The young technician tried a professional smile and failed at it midway.
\par \'93It\rquote s only metadata.\'94
\par \'93No,\'94 said Fainrose. \'93That is what foolish people call reality when they are too cowardly to say they have abstracted it.\'94
\par Beatrix was already scanning the local logs.
\par \'93How long have they known about the thermal rise.\'94
\par The technician hesitated.
\par Jago leaned on the console. \'93I ask this as a friend of institutional collapse: answer quickly.\'94
\par \'93Three months,\'94 said the man.
\par Beatrix looked up.
\par \'93Three months.\'94
\par \'93It was judged non-critical.\'94
\par Fainrose gave a short laugh devoid of amusement.
\par \'93Of course it was. Slow disasters are irresistible to committees.\'94
\par Neville was no longer looking at the technicians. He was watching the transport map. Each time one route vanished, a line elsewhere brightened. Each time a domestic event entered the overflow channel, the anchor field fluctuated by a tiny amount. Separate
ly, none of it meant much. Together, it produced a pattern so offensively neat that he felt insulted on behalf of causality.
\par \'93These aren\rquote t separate failures,\'94 he said.
\par \'93No,\'94 said Fainrose.
\par \'93The routes, the queue, the houses\emdash \'94
\par \'93No.\'94
\par \'93They\rquote re coupled.\'94
\par Fainrose came to stand beside him.
\par \'93Yes.\'94
\par On the lower part of the screen, hidden behind system tabs, was a label he had not noticed before:
\par CORRELATION BUDGET
\par Beneath it, a meter was moving steadily toward red.
\par \'93What,\'94 said Neville, \'93is a correlation budget.\'94
\par The room became quiet in the way rooms do when language has at last chosen to become incriminating.
\par Fainrose folded her arms.
\par \'93It means,\'94 she said, \'93that some administrative ghoul decided interconnectedness was too expensive to maintain in full.\'94
\par Mrs Chain frowned. \'93Interconnectedness of what.\'94
\par \'93Lives. records. places. choices. Shared histories. Mutual constraints. Any detail whose meaning depends on something else.\'94
\par Jago considered this. \'93So instead of managing complexity, they\rquote ve been charging it rent.\'94
\par \'93Precisely.\'94
\par The technician, perhaps sensing a need to defend the dead, said, \'93The system only suppresses non-essential linkages.\'94
\par Mrs Chain turned to him with the calm of a woman who had already passed through outrage and was now travelling under clearer weather.
\par \'93My sister,\'94 she said, \'93is not a linkage.\'94
\par Nobody improved on this.
\par Fainrose reached over the console and opened a deeper systems pane. The diagram that appeared looked less like software than a nervous breakdown rendered in geometry: district nodes, domestic nodes, routing lattices, and at the centre a black disk marked 
4C, around which thousands of fine lines converged and vanished.
\par Neville stared.
\par \'93They attached everything to it.\'94
\par \'93Not everything,\'94 said Fainrose. \'93Only everything they meant to simplify.\'94
\par \'93And now it\rquote s evaporating.\'94
\par \'93Yes.\'94
\par \'93So the things tied to it don\rquote t just break. They become uncertain together.\'94
\par \'93Exactly.\'94
\par One of the senior technicians entered at that moment, carrying a clipboard and the settled confidence of a man who had survived many meetings by confusing sequence with thought.
\par \'93What is going on here.\'94
\par Beatrix held up her identification.
\par \'93Official review.\'94
\par \'93We have no notice of review.\'94
\par \'93You do now.\'94
\par He took this badly and looked to the monitors for institutional support. Instead he found Fainrose.
\par His face altered.
\par \'93Dr Fainrose.\'94
\par \'93Mr Voller.\'94
\par \'93I was told you had become private.\'94
\par \'93And I was told you had become harmless. We are both disappointed.\'94
\par Voller drew himself up.
\par \'93The anchor remains within tolerance.\'94
\par Fainrose pointed at the red-trending mass curve.
\par \'93No. The software remains within tolerance. The black hole is evaporating.\'94
\par He smiled the smile of a career bureaucrat greeting an inconvenient noun.
\par \'93That interpretation is contested.\'94
\par \'93It is thermodynamics,\'94 said Fainrose. \'93Not a consultation.\'94
\par Neville almost wrote that down and then realised he had already started.
\par Voller turned to the correlation display. \'93The coupling lattice is robust.\'94
\par Fainrose\rquote s expression became almost kind, which made it much worse.
\par \'93Yes,\'94 she said. \'93That is the problem. The more robust the entanglement, the less politely the decoherence spreads.\'94
\par Voller blinked. \'93The what spreads.\'94
\par Now Neville was listening with his whole body.
\par Fainrose tapped the central black disk.
\par \'93
You tied many local systems to one central quantum-gravitational anchor. That means their states are no longer fully independent. They share structure. Once the anchor becomes noisy, hot, or unstable, the environment starts learning which alternatives ar
e linked to which. Coherence drains away. The system stops behaving like one carefully maintained possibility and starts behaving like a classical mess.\'94
\par The technician at the near console said, \'93That sounds speculative.\'94
\par \'93It sounds,\'94 said Fainrose, \'93like you were never taught any quantum mechanics.\'94
\par Neville looked back at the diagram.
\par \'93Entanglement,\'94 he said slowly, \'93means you can\rquote t describe the parts independently anymore.\'94
\par \'93Yes.\'94
\par \'93And decoherence means the environment starts carrying away the phase relations.\'94
\par \'93Yes.\'94
\par \'93So once that happens, the lost alternatives don\rquote t recombine cleanly.\'94
\par \'93No.\'94
\par \'93They become records of damage.\'94
\par Fainrose glanced at him, and for a moment there was something almost approving in her face.
\par \'93Very good.\'94
\par Mrs Chain surveyed the room, the screens, the men, the language, and the trembling civic skeleton of the thing.
\par \'93In plain English,\'94 she said.
\par Fainrose obliged.
\par \'93They have connected too many delicate realities to one dying object. Now that object is leaking order.\'94
\par \'93That,\'94 said Mrs Chain, \'93is plain enough.\'94
\par On the main display, the correlation budget crossed into red.
\par Every monitor in the room flickered once.
\par Then again.
\par Across the transport map, three routes vanished simultaneously. The domestic-events stream began scrolling too fast to read. Somewhere deeper in the annex, an alarm started and then stopped, as if embarrassed to have noticed reality before management had 
approved the event.
\par Voller turned pale.
\par \'93What did you do.\'94
\par Fainrose did not move.
\par \'93I arrived,\'94 she said. \'93The universe did the rest.\'94
\par Neville was watching one smaller pane on the right-hand side of the screen. It listed overflow returns. Items that had been channelled into the anchor were beginning, for want of a better term, to come back.
\par Not whole.
\par Fragments.
\par Alternative street names. Contradictory birth records. old queue tokens. ownership disputes. duplicate memorial photographs. rejected room layouts. mislaid correspondences. A hundred small amputations of the ordinary, all pressing at once against the weak
ened civic membrane.
\par \'93They\rquote re coming out,\'94 he said.
\par \'93They were never gone,\'94 said Fainrose. \'93They were only stored somewhere obscene.\'94
\par Beatrix had already moved to the emergency controls.
\par \'93Can we isolate districts.\'94
\par \'93Not without making choices,\'94 said Fainrose.
\par \'93What choices.\'94
\par \'93Which correlations to save.\'94
\par Nobody enjoyed that sentence.
\par Jago looked at the map and whistled softly.
\par \'93Well,\'94 he said, \'93we seem at last to have found the national character: underfunded entanglement.\'94
\par Voller was shouting into a desk phone now, demanding senior clearance from people who were almost certainly already practising distance.
\par The screens flashed again.
\par This time, Neville felt it.
\par Not physically, exactly. More as a brief rearrangement in the logic of nearby things, as if every object in the room had been forced to remember what else it depended on. He saw Mrs Chain grip the umbrella, saw Beatrix brace one hand on the console, saw J
ago step back from a monitor that had begun listing addresses in two different counties at once.
\par And then, on the central display, a new warning appeared:
\par ANCHOR COHERENCE LOSS CASCADING\line LOCAL RECOVERY NO LONGER COMMUTATIVE
\par Neville stared at the last word.
\par Fainrose saw it too.
\par \'93Oh, excellent,\'94 she said without enthusiasm. \'93Order now matters.\'94
\par Mrs Chain said, \'93I beg your pardon.\'94
\par \'93It means,\'94 said Neville, before he could stop himself, \'93that you can\rquote t fix everything in any sequence you like. If two repairs don\rquote t commute, doing A then B gives a different result from doing B then A.\'94
\par Fainrose looked at him.
\par \'93Yes,\'94 she said. \'93And now you see why I never sleep.\'94
\par Beatrix turned from the controls.
\par \'93Can we still stop the cascade.\'94
\par Fainrose\rquote s answer was immediate.
\par \'93No.\'94
\par The room held that.
\par Then she added, \'93But we may still choose its shape.\'94
\par That was the point, Neville realised, at which disaster ceased to be theoretical and became administration again, though of a more honest and terrible sort.
\par Beatrix straightened.
\par \'93Right,\'94 she said. \'93Then we prioritise.\'94
\par Mrs Chain lifted her umbrella.
\par \'93At last,\'94 she said, \'93a meeting with a moral centre.\'94
\par Fainrose began issuing instructions so quickly that even panic had to become organised in order to keep up.
\par \'93Freeze new convergence events. Cut overflow routing. Detach non-essential transport couplings. Preserve hospitals, archives, and power lattices. Anything sentimental with structural dependency goes above policy threshold.\'94
\par Voller stared. \'93Sentimental.\'94
\par \'93Yes,\'94 snapped Fainrose. \'93That is where people keep the irreplaceable pieces, Mr Voller. We have been over this as a civilisation.\'94
\par Neville opened his notebook to a clean page and wrote:
\par Entangled systems fail together.\line Decohered systems do not forgive sequence.
\par Then, beneath it:
\par The country is attached to a dying argument with physics.
