\par Decoherence is the loss of quantum coherence when a system interacts with its environment, and it is commonly described as information about the system leaking into the environment.\line 
In the density-matrix picture, decoherence shows up as the decay of the off-diagonal terms, which is why interference between alternatives disappears and the system starts looking classical rather than delicately quantum.
\par A key point is that decoherence does not by itself create a literal wave-function collapse; instead, it explains the appearance of collapse because the system becomes entangled with many environmental degrees of freedom and interference becomes effectivel
y inaccessible.\line That is the engine under this chapter: once the anchor becomes noisy and the environment \'93learns\'94 too much, the suppressed alternatives cannot recombine neatly, so recovery becomes path-dependent and messy.
