There is a particular species of official calm that arrives only after the situation has become unsalvageable.
\par It is not courage. It is not competence. It is the calm of a man who has just realised that the thing he is standing in will shortly be described in a report, and who has begun, quietly and with great professional discipline, to consider his own paragraph
.
\par Mr Voller had reached this stage by twenty past four.
\par "There is a restoration protocol," he said.
\par The Regional Compression Annex had by then acquired the atmosphere of a hospital in which the patient had begun offering opinions. Two of the wall displays were showing districts that did not currently agree on their own boundaries. A printer in the corne
r had produced eleven copies of a form that had never been designed. Somewhere below them, in the deeper part of the building where the anchor housing sat behind three doors and a good deal of optimism, something was making a sound not unlike a cathedral 
clearing its throat.
\par Stephanie Fainrose did not look up from the console.
\par "Say that again," she said.
\par Voller straightened his cuffs, which is what such men do instead of praying.
\par "There is a restoration protocol," he repeated. "Full-state civic reconstruction. We hold a reference copy."
\par Beatrix Sloan turned very slowly.
\par "You hold what."
\par "A reference copy," said Voller. "Of the pre-simplification configuration. Homes, records, routes, identity linkages. Everything the Initiative touched was mirrored before adjustment. It was a condition of approval. I signed the assurance myself."
\par Neville Wren, who had spent the last hour watching reality itemise its own injuries, felt a brief and treacherous flare of hope. It lasted approximately as long as it took him to look at Fainrose's face.
\par She had gone quite still.
\par "Mr Voller," she said, and her voice had lost all its edges, which was infinitely worse than when it had them, "where is this copy."
\par "Cold vault. Sub-level two. Independent substrate, isolated field, no live coupling." He permitted himself the smallest recovery of dignity. "Precisely so that it could not be affected by anything occurring above."
\par "And what," said Fainrose, "is in it."
\par Voller blinked. "The state. The prior state."
\par "Which prior state."
\par "The correct one."
\par Fainrose closed her eyes for a moment.
\par "Neville," she said. "Come here."
\par He went.
\par She pulled a fresh pane onto the display and typed a short command. The vault manifest opened: page after page of entries, each one a district, a street, a dwelling, a person, a linkage, all neatly indexed, all timestamped, all marked with a small green t
ick indicating successful capture.
\par It was, Neville thought, one of the most reassuring documents he had ever seen, and he understood at once that this was the problem with it.
\par "Look at the fidelity column," said Fainrose.
\par He looked.
\par STATE CAPTURE: COMPLETE\line CLASSICAL REPRESENTATION: VERIFIED\line PHASE RECORD: NOT APPLICABLE
\par He read the last line three times, in the manner of a man hoping that words might be persuaded.
\par "Not applicable," he said.
\par "Not applicable," said Fainrose.
\par "They didn't record the phase relations."
\par "They could not record the phase relations."
\par "Then this isn't a copy."
\par "No," said Fainrose. "It is a photograph of a conversation."
\par Mrs Elspeth Chain had come to the console with her umbrella in the crook of her arm and the expression of a woman determined to be present at the moment when the men admitted it.
\par "Explain," she said.
\par Fainrose turned to her, and something in her manner changed. She spoke to Mrs Chain, always, as though Mrs Chain were the only person in the room whose understanding actually mattered \emdash 
 which, Neville had begun to suspect, was a fairly accurate assessment.
\par "When they measured your house," said Fainrose, "in order to save it, they had to ask it questions."
\par "They asked a great many."
\par "Yes. And each question returned an answer. Blue wallpaper. One duvet. Husband present. Sister absent. All perfectly recordable. All faithfully written down." She tapped the manifest. "That is what is in the vault. Answers."
\par "And that is not the house."
\par "That is not the house. The house was also the relations between the answers. The way the sister depended on the kitchen, and the kitchen on the route to the shops, and the route on forty years of walking it. Those relations were not answers to any questi
on they knew how to ask. To write them down, they would have had to ask a different question \emdash  and asking it would have destroyed the answers they had already taken."
\par Mrs Chain considered this for some time.
\par "So they could have one or the other," she said.
\par "Yes."
\par "But not both."
\par "Not both. Never both. Not by incompetence \emdash  by law." Fainrose's mouth tightened. "And so, being administrators, they took the one that fitted the form."
\par Jago Flint, from his position against the doorframe, said, "Forgive me. Could they not simply have made a second house and asked it the other question."
\par "No," said Fainrose.
\par "Why not."
\par "Because you cannot copy it, Mr Flint. That is not a difficulty. That is a theorem."
\par Voller made a small noise of protest. "We back up systems constantly. Every department\emdash "
\par "You back up records," said Fainrose. "You have never once backed up a state. Nobody has. Nobody will. If a general quantum state could be duplicated, you could measure the copy without disturbing the original, and the entire structure of physics would co
me apart in your hands like wet cardboard. It cannot be done. It has been known that it cannot be done for a very long time. It is in the literature. It is not obscure." She looked at him. "You signed an assurance that you had done an impossible thing, an
d nobody in the approval chain knew enough to laugh."
\par The silence that followed had a specific quality. It was the silence of five people all doing the same arithmetic and arriving at the same figure.
\par Neville broke it. "Then the vault is worthless."
\par "No," said Fainrose. "Worthless would be a mercy. The vault is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14165747\charrsid14165747 plausible}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14165747\charrsid14165747 
. Which means that at some point in the next several hours, a senior person will be told that a full restoration is available, and that person will authorise it, and the system will overwrite what remains of the real configuration with a flattened cartoon
 of it, and it will report success in green letters."
\par Beatrix had already moved. "Then we lock the vault."
\par "Yes."
\par "On what authority."
\par "Mine," said Beatrix, "retroactively."
\par Voller found his voice. "You cannot seal a national restoration asset on a theoretical objection."
\par Beatrix looked at him with the particular patience of an auditor who has been waiting eleven years for exactly this sentence.
\par "Mr Voller," she said, "I have sealed a hospital laundry contract over a missing signature. You have no idea what I am capable of when the physics is on my side."
\par She began typing.
\par Fainrose, meanwhile, had turned back to the anchor traces, and Neville \emdash  who had learned over the past day to read her shoulders the way sailors read weather \emdash  saw that she had stopped being angry, which meant she had started being useful.

\par "If we cannot restore it," he said, "what can we do."
\par "Something better," said Fainrose. "Something the vault people never understood, because they were thinking about storage instead of structure." She pulled up the correlation lattice again: the great trembling web of district nodes, domestic nodes, routin
g lines, all of it still convergent on the black disk marked 4C. "Look at it properly, Neville. Not as a map. As a code."
\par He looked.
\par "There's redundancy," he said slowly. "The same information appears in more than one place. Mrs Chain's sister isn't only in Mrs Chain's house. She's in the ownership record, and the queue token, and the memorial photograph, and the route to the shops, an
d the two contradictory birth entries\emdash "
\par "Go on."
\par "\emdash and no single one of those is sufficient. But together they overdetermine her." He felt the shape of it arrive. "You don't need the copy. The information isn't in any one place. It's in the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14165747\charrsid14165747 pattern across all of them.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14165747\charrsid14165747 "
\par Fainrose permitted herself, at last, the full smile. It was brief, and slightly frightening, and Neville understood that he had just passed an examination he had not known he was sitting.
\par "That," she said, "is how one protects a delicate thing without ever holding it. You do not keep a copy. You spread it out so thin that no local damage can find enough of it to destroy. Any single node may be lost, corrupted, misfiled, or run over by a lo
rry, and the whole may still be recovered from what remains \emdash  provided you know which correlations to trust, and provided you repair them in the right order."
\par Jago said, "And do we know the right order."
\par "No."
\par "Ah."
\par "But we know it exists," said Fainrose, "which is more than the Ministry has known about anything since Tuesday."
\par Beatrix finished typing and stood back. On the main display, the words RESTORATION ASSET: SEALED PENDING REVIEW appeared, followed almost immediately by three separate priority queries from three separate departments, all of which she ignored with the ser
enity of a woman who had already decided which hill this was.
\par "How long to work out the order," she said.
\par Fainrose was already assembling something on the second console \emdash  a syndrome table, Neville realised, watching the columns fill: not a list of what was broken, but a list of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14165747\charrsid14165747 which combinations of breakages could be told apart from one another.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14165747\charrsid14165747 
\par "That depends," she said, "on whether we can measure the damage without measuring the thing."
\par Neville stared at her. "Is that possible."
\par "It is the only thing that is possible," said Fainrose. "You do not ask the patient how they are. You ask whether their left hand and right hand still agree. The answer tells you where the injury is and nothing whatever about who they are \emdash 
 which is precisely why it does not hurt them." She glanced up. "It is the one form of inspection this Ministry never learned, because it requires wanting to know something other than an answer."
\par Below them, in the anchor housing, the cathedral cleared its throat again.
\par Mrs Chain looked at the ceiling, then at the floor, then at Fainrose.
\par "Dr Fainrose."
\par "Mrs Chain."
\par "When you have finished," said Mrs Chain, "will my sister be my sister."
\par Fainrose did not soften it, and Neville thought better of her for it.
\par "She will be recoverable," she said. "That is not the same as unharmed. But it is the difference between a person and a form, and I intend to spend the rest of the evening defending it."
\par Mrs Chain nodded once.
\par "Then I shall make the tea," she said, "and you will all stop apologising and start working."
\par Neville opened the green notebook.
\par He wrote:
\par No copy exists. No copy can exist.
\par Then, below it:
\par But it is written everywhere at once, faintly,\line and that is stronger than a copy \emdash \line provided one repairs it in the right order.
\par And then, after a moment, in smaller letters:
\par They built a vault.\line They should have built a code.
