\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16412321 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
The order came down at six minutes past nine the following morning, on paper, which was itself a bad sign.
\par Ministries that intend to be reasonable send email. Email can be amended, forwarded, quietly forgotten. Paper is sent when someone senior wishes there to be an object.
\par Beatrix Sloan read it twice at the annex reception desk, then carried it to the long table in the observation room and put it down in front of Stephanie Fainrose without comment.
\par Fainrose read it. Then she laughed \emdash  one short, unlovely sound, like a chair leg on tile.
\par "Oh, they've done it," she said. "They've actually gone and legislated it."
\par Neville Wren leaned over. The document ran to a page and a half and had been through at least four hands, each of which had made it worse.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 DIRECTIVE 11-C: CIVIC RECOVERY ASSURANCE}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
In order that public confidence be maintained during the current period of adjustment, all recovery operations shall be conducted so as to satisfy both of the following conditions simultaneously:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
(a) The precise present configuration of each affected dwelling, record, and person shall be established and certified to district level, such that no citizen enquiry may receive an indeterminate response.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
(b) The precise rate and direction of ongoing change in each affected dwelling, record, and person shall be established and certified to district level, such that all future states may be projected and no citizen enquiry may receive an unforecastable resp
onse.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 Recovery may not proceed until both certificates are held.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 Signed, on behalf of the Board,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 \line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 C. Vale}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
\par Neville read it again.
\par "Both," he said.
\par "Both," said Fainrose. "To arbitrary precision. Simultaneously. With certificates."
\par "That's\emdash "
\par "Yes."
\par "That's not a difficult instruction."
\par "No," said Fainrose. "It is not a difficult instruction. A difficult instruction is one you fail. This is an instruction you cannot attempt." She set the paper down very precisely, squaring it to the edge of the table, which Neville had learned was what s
he did instead of throwing things. "Somebody has looked at physics, disliked the terms, and issued a rebuttal."
\par Jago Flint, who had spent the night in a chair and looked entirely unaffected by it in a way Neville found personally insulting, said, "Is it truly impossible, or only very hard. I ask because I have made a living from the gap."
\par "There is no gap."
\par "There is always a gap."
\par "Not here." Fainrose reached for a blackboard on castors and dragged it round, and something in the room's temperature changed; even Voller, hovering by the wall, stopped pretending to consult his clipboard. "Mr Flint. If I wish to know exactly }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 where}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  a thing is, what must I do to it."
\par Jago considered. "Pin it."
\par "Yes. Confine it. Narrow it down. Insist on a location and refuse alternatives." She drew a single sharp vertical stroke. "And what have I then done to its }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 motion}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 ."
\par "Made it\emdash " He stopped. "Made it jumpy."
\par "Made it wild," said Fainrose. "Not because I was clumsy. Because the sharper the position, the broader the range of motions consistent with it. The two are not two separate facts about the object that I happen to be measuring badly. They are one fact, re
ad in two directions, and the directions trade against each other. Squeeze one and the other spreads. There is a floor beneath the product, and no apparatus, budget, or signature gets under it."
\par Mrs Elspeth Chain, who had appointed herself to the tea and to the moral summary, said, "And they have ordered both."
\par "To district level."
\par "With certificates."
\par "With certificates."
\par Mrs Chain set down the pot. "Then they have ordered the tide."
\par "Considerably worse," said Fainrose. "The tide would refuse and go on being the tide. A civil service does not refuse. It complies."
\par Neville felt the floor of the argument give slightly beneath him.
\par "What does compliance look like," he said, "when the thing is impossible."
\par Fainrose looked at him for a long moment.
\par "That," she said, "is the only question in the directive."
\par The answer arrived at ten forty, in the form of a young woman from Certification named Miss Adaeze Pike, who had a laptop, a lanyard, and the specific brightness of a person doing well within a system she has not yet examined.
\par She was, Neville thought, entirely blameless, and he disliked what was about to happen to her.
\par "We've had 11-C too," she said cheerfully, setting up at the end of the table. "Field Certification. We've been running since seven."
\par Beatrix's head came up. "Running what."
\par "Both columns." Miss Pike turned the laptop so they could see. "Column A is present configuration. Column B is rate of change. Every affected address gets a line."
\par The spreadsheet was beautiful. Neville had been in the electronics trade for a long time and he knew a well-built sheet when he saw one \emdash  conditional formatting, locked headers, validation on every field. Somebody had cared about this.
\par Every single row was filled.
\par "How," said Fainrose.
\par Miss Pike blinked. "How what, sorry?"
\par "How did you fill Column B."
\par "Oh \emdash 
 from the survey," said Miss Pike. "The Continuity Review data. We take the certified present configuration, compare it to the previous certified present configuration, divide by the interval. That gives rate of change." She smiled, pleased with the 
tidiness of it. "It's rather elegant, actually. The same measurement serves both columns."
\par There was a pause of perhaps three seconds.
\par Then Fainrose said, very gently, "Miss Pike, would you sit down."
\par Miss Pike sat down, because that was the sort of voice it had been.
\par "When you certified the present configuration," said Fainrose, "what did the surveyor do to the dwelling."
\par "Scanned it. Object by object. It's a full resolution pass, so every element is fixed to certified state \emdash  that's what makes Column A reliable."
\par "Fixed."
\par "Yes."
\par "So at the moment of the scan, the dwelling was as narrowly determined as your instrument could make it."
\par "That's the standard, yes."
\par "And then you scanned it again a week later, and compared."
\par "Exactly."
\par "And you divided the difference by seven days," said Fainrose, "and wrote the result in Column B, and called it the rate of change of the house."
\par "Yes."
\par "Miss Pike," said Fainrose, "you have not measured the rate of change of the house. You have measured the rate at which your own instrument disturbed it."
\par Miss Pike's expression did not change immediately. Neville watched it fail to change, and then watched it change.
\par "No," she said. "No, because the disturbance is calibrated out. There's a correction factor. Annex four."
\par "Show me annex four."
\par She showed her. Fainrose read it in about eleven seconds, and Neville, who had by now developed a reasonable ear for her silences, understood that the eleven seconds were courtesy.
\par "This factor," said Fainrose, "was derived by comparing scan results against a reference configuration."
\par "Yes."
\par "Held in the cold vault."
\par Miss Pike's fingers stopped on the trackpad.
\par "The vault's been sealed since last night," she said. "There's a note. I assumed it was procedural."
\par "It was me," said Beatrix.
\par "Why?"
\par "Because it is a photograph of a conversation," said Beatrix, "and I did not intend to let anyone restore the country from it."
\par Miss Pike looked at the spreadsheet. She looked at it for rather a long time. Neville, who had spent forty years watching people discover that the ground was not where the drawing said it was, recognised every stage of it and could not think of a single k
indness to offer.
\par "Then Column B is\emdash " She stopped and started again, and got it out properly the second time, which he respected. "Then Column B is the instrument looking at itself."
\par "Yes," said Fainrose.
\par "Nine thousand rows."
\par "Yes."
\par "And Column A is real."
\par "Column A is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 an answer}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
," said Fainrose. "It is real in the way a verdict is real. It became true when you asked."
\par Miss Pike put both hands flat on the table.
\par "So the more precisely we certified where things were," she said slowly, "the more nonsense we generated about where they were going."
\par "Yes."
\par "And the directive requires both."
\par "Yes."
\par "So the harder we comply, the worse it gets."
\par "Now," said Fainrose, "you have understood 11-C better than the man who signed it."
\par It was Mrs Chain who asked the question that mattered, because it was always Mrs Chain who asked the question that mattered.
\par "What has it done to the people," she said.
\par Nobody answered.
\par "Dr Fainrose. Nine thousand rows. They are addresses. Somebody lives in each of them." She folded her hands. "What has it done to them."
\par Fainrose crossed to the main display and pulled the domestic event stream, and filtered it, and Neville saw her hesitate before she let it render \emdash  the first time he had seen her flinch from her own data.
\par The pattern was unmistakable once you knew to look for it.
\par Every dwelling that had been certified to the highest precision in Column A showed, in the days following, the wildest instability in every other respect. Routes to the shops that changed on alternate mornings. Correspondence arriving addressed to residen
ts who were and were not the residents. Two households whose front doors had, for a period of eleven hours, both opened onto the same hallway, an incident logged as }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
shared vestibule, low priority.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  
A man in Sub-District 9 who had been certified so exactly that his own employment record could no longer predict where he would be at nine in the morning, and who had accordingly been marked unreliable.
\par "They pinned them," said Neville.
\par "They pinned them," said Fainrose, "and then wrote down that they were erratic. And the more certain the pin, the more erratic the record, and the more erratic the record, the stronger the case for a further pin." She turned. "It is a machine for manufact
uring exactly the instability it was built to remove, and it will run for as long as it is funded, because every cycle produces evidence that it is needed."
\par Jago exhaled. "That is the most administratively perfect thing I have ever heard."
\par "It is not a flaw in the Initiative," said Fainrose. "It is the Initiative. Vale does not need reality to be simple. He needs it to be }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 reportably}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  simple. Those are different projects, and only one of them is achievable, and it is achieved by insisting on two certificates that cannot both be honest."
\par Beatrix had gone very quiet, which in her case meant she had stopped being furious and started constructing.
\par "Miss Pike," she said.
\par Miss Pike looked up.
\par "Column B is fabricated."
\par "Not deliberately\emdash "
\par "I did not say fraudulently. I said fabricated. Do you dispute it."
\par A long pause. Then, to her lasting credit: "No. I don't."
\par "Will you say so in writing."
\par Miss Pike looked at the spreadsheet \emdash  nine thousand rows, locked headers, conditional formatting, four weeks of good work in service of an impossibility \emdash 
 and Neville watched her decide, and thought that whatever else came of this appalling week, that had been worth being present for.
\par "Yes," she said.
\par "Then we have him," said Beatrix. "Not for the black hole. Not for the houses. Nobody is ever hanged for a black hole." She picked up the directive and held it up. "For requiring, in writing, over signature, a certification that cannot exist \emdash 
 and then acting on it. That is not a scientific dispute. That is a false instrument in a live system."
\par Fainrose looked at the paper with something like respect.
\par "He wrote it down," she said.
\par "He wrote it down," said Beatrix.
\par Mrs Chain refilled the pot.
\par "Men like that always do," she said. "They cannot bear a thing to be true without a copy."
\par Neville opened the green notebook.
\par He wrote:
\par You may know where it is.\line You may know where it is going.\line You may not have both, and the shortfall is not a shortage of effort.
\par Then, below:
\par They demanded both.\line So the second column had to be invented.\line And then they punished people for failing to match the invention.
\par And at the bottom, smaller:
\par The cruelty is not the uncertainty.\line The cruelty is the certificate.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16412321 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs36\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
Quantum mechanics
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16412321 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 This chapter teaches the }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 uncertainty principle}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 , and specifically the thing about it that is most often got wrong.
\par The popular version says that measurement is clumsy \emdash 
 that our instruments jog the particle and blur the result, and that a sufficiently delicate apparatus would eventually get around it. That version is comfortable, and wrong, and it is the version Directive 11-C is built on.
\par The actual statement is structural, not practical. Position and momentum are }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 conjugate}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  
quantities: they are not two independent properties that a system separately possesses, but two complementary readings of a single underlying state. A state sharply defined in position is necessarily a broad superposition in momentum, and vice versa 
\emdash  mathematically, the two descriptions are Fourier transforms of one another, and no function can be narrow in both domains at once. The relation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 \\Delta x\\,\\
Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  is therefore a floor on the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 product}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  of the spreads, imposed by what the state is, not by how carefully you look at it.
\par This is why the story's cruelty works. The surveyors were not sloppy. They were excellent. Their excellence at Column A is precisely what guaranteed the wildness that got written into Column B \emdash 
 and because they then divided successive sharp measurements to infer a rate, they were reading their own imposed collapses and calling it the behaviour of the house.
\par Two further points sit underneath the chapter:
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls22\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid10895145 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 Complementarity is not ignorance.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  The unpinned quantity is not a value that exists and is merely unknown. Insisting otherwise is the error that produces the fabricated column.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
Conjugate demands are politically exploitable.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  A requirement to certify two incompatible quantities to arbitrary precision cannot be met, but it can be }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 complied with}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321  \emdash 
 and the compliance will consist of invented numbers, which are then treated as findings about the citizens they were invented about.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16412321 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs27 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs27\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 
What the reader learns
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls23\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid10895145 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 Uncertainty is a property of nature, not of laboratories.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Sharpening one quantity necessarily broadens its conjugate.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Inferring a rate from repeated sharp measurements measures the measuring.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16412321\charrsid16412321 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}
An institution that demands the impossible does not get the impossible; it gets fiction, plus a mechanism for blaming people for not matching it.
