An Uninvited Reflection
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14114306 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
The Phase Two papers arrived at two, in eleven boxes, and Beatrix Sloan had read four of them by half past five with the expression of a woman conducting a post-mortem on someone she had known.
\par Neville Wren had been given box seven, which contained the consent architecture, and it was in box seven that he found the thing that made him go and stand outside for a while.
\par "It's the invitation," he said, when he came back in.
\par Fainrose looked up. "Say that again."
\par "Phase Two doesn't take memories." He put the folder on the table and opened it to the tab he had turned down. "It can't. Not directly. There's an entire volume here on why not \emdash 
 some poor devil in Legal wrote it in 2019 and nobody read it. A memory can't be read out by external survey. It's not stored anywhere accessible. It only becomes definite when the person is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 asked}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 ."
\par Beatrix said, "So they ask."
\par "So they ask," said Neville. "And the asking is the whole mechanism. The Ministry can't reach in. But if a citizen fills in a form \emdash  describes the memory, selects from options, signs the declaration \emdash 
 then the memory becomes whatever the form says, because the form was the measurement." He turned the folder round. "Look at the title of the volume."
\par Fainrose read it aloud. "}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 Voluntary Recollection Assurance: Establishing Lawful Access Through Citizen-Initiated Enquiry.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 "
\par "It can't come in," said Neville, "unless you invite it."
\par Jago Flint, who had been asleep in the chair again and had apparently been listening through it, said without opening his eyes, "Oh, that's an old shape."
\par "What is."
\par "That." He sat up. "Can't cross the threshold uninvited. Very old. Doesn't usually turn up in policy documents."
\par It was at this point that the lamps dimmed by one degree, in a manner Neville had last seen in Mrs Chain's hallway, and which he had learned to distrust profoundly.
\par "Ah," said a voice from the doorway. "Forgive me. I did knock, but the door was in two minds."
\par The man standing there was extremely old and radiantly untidy.
\par He had a great disorganised nimbus of white hair, a moustache of magnificent indifference, a cardigan that had clearly outlived several arguments, and no shoes. His face was one that every person in the room had seen a hundred times on posters, mugs, moti
vational calendars, and the walls of secondary-school physics laboratories, and the resemblance was so total, so unembarrassed, that it produced not recognition but a sort of vertigo.
\par He beamed at them.
\par "Halpert Weinstein," he said. "Please don't."
\par "Don't what," said Neville.
\par "Say it. Everybody says it. I have had eleven decades of it and it was tiresome by 1931." He came in without hurrying \emdash 
 and Neville noticed that the doorframe seemed to accommodate him rather than the reverse. "No relation. Well. Not in the sense you mean by relation."
\par Fainrose had risen from her chair.
\par "Dr Fainrose," said Weinstein warmly. "I have read you. The correlation paper. Beautiful. Wrong in one place, which we shall come to, but beautiful."
\par "Where wrong."
\par "Later. You will enjoy it more when you are frightened." He looked round the room with enormous, benevolent interest. "Ms Sloan. Mr Flint. Mrs Chain \emdash 
 my condolences on your sister and my congratulations on your umbrella. Mr Wren." He stopped. "Mr Wren, you have found the invitation clause."
\par "About ten minutes ago."
\par "Yes," said Weinstein. "That is when I could come."
\par Nobody moved.
\par Mrs Elspeth Chain, who had by now developed a settled policy of addressing the impossible directly and letting it explain itself, said, "Who are you."
\par Weinstein considered the question with visible relish, like a man choosing from a good cellar.
\par "I am the oldest consequence," he said. "There is no better phrase. Some have tried. The Babylonians had one but it doesn't translate."
\par Beatrix said, "That is not an answer."
\par "No. Try this." He crossed to the long window, and the late sun came through it and lay on the floor in a rhombus, and he stepped into the middle of the rhombus and stood there.
\par He cast no shadow.
\par Neville looked at the floor for a considerable time. The chairs had shadows. The table had a shadow. The old man in the cardigan stood in full unmediated August light and the floor beneath him was as bright as the floor beside him.
\par "There," said Weinstein comfortably. "That usually does it."
\par Jago said, "You'll want the mirror as well."
\par "You are a quick young man and I shall try to keep you alive." Weinstein turned to the glass-fronted cabinet on the far wall, in which every one of them was reflected, and the sunlight, and the doorway, and the eleven boxes.
\par He was not.
\par Mrs Chain took a firm hold of the back of a chair.
\par "You are a vampire," she said.
\par "That is the local word and I have stopped resisting it," said Weinstein. "It is roughly as accurate as calling the sun a lamp. But yes. Approximately. Functionally. For our purposes this afternoon: yes."
\par "Then what do you drink."
\par And Weinstein's beam faded \emdash  not into menace, but into something far worse, which was seriousness.
\par "Ah," he said. "You go straight to it. Good. I do so dislike the ones who ask about bats."
\par He came away from the window and sat down, uninvited, in the chair Jago had vacated.
\par "I do not drink blood, Mrs Chain. Blood is a courier. It carries the thing but it is not the thing." He laced his old spotted hands together. "Consider what happens when a question is put to the world, and the world \emdash 
 which had been holding several answers at once, quite comfortably, at no cost to anyone \emdash  is obliged to produce one. The other answers do not go anywhere. They are not }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 filed}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
. They cease, and in ceasing they release something." He shrugged, almost apologetically. "I take that. I have taken it since the first measurement, which was much longer ago than your people think and was not performed by anybody."
\par Neville felt the room tilt.
\par "You feed on collapse."
\par "On the discarded branch, yes. The road not taken, as your poet had it \emdash  though he was sentimental and I am a diner."
\par "Then\emdash " Neville's voice came out wrong and he started again. "Then the Simplification Initiative\emdash "
\par "Is the finest thing that has happened to me since agriculture," said Halpert Weinstein.
\par The silence that followed had a physical weight.
\par "Nine thousand dwellings," he said, "each one forced from a rich state into a thin one, tens of thousands of alternatives extinguished per address, per week, at public expense, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 with certificates}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
. Do you understand what that is, from where I sit? It is not a meal. A meal is a woman deciding at last which of two men she loved and letting the other life go. Sweet. Small. I have lived on such things for aeons and been perfectly content." He spread h
is hands. "This is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 industry}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 . This is a national programme with a budget line."
\par Fainrose said, very carefully, "And the anchor. 4C."
\par Weinstein's expression changed for the first time.
\par "No," he said. "That is not mine, and I want it understood. I did not ask them to build a sink and I did not ask them to hang a county from an evaporating hole. That is not appetite, Dr Fainrose, that is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 waste}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
, and it will end with the pantry on fire." He sat forward. "This is why I have come. Not to gloat. I could have gloated from Vienna."
\par "Then why."
\par "Because Phase Two is a mistake even by my standards," said Weinstein, "and because I appear to be, in this room, the only person old enough to be genuinely conservative about reality."
\par He picked up box seven's folder and turned it over with distaste.
\par "Houses I can dine on for a century. But memory is not a house. A memory is not one branch among several \emdash  it is the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 record}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306  of which branch was taken. It is how the universe keeps its accounts." He tapped the cover. 
"If you begin collapsing memory by questionnaire, you are not extinguishing alternatives. You are falsifying the ledger of which alternatives were extinguished. And then, my dears, nobody can tell what happened, including me, including the thing that come
s after me, including \emdash  and this should worry you most \emdash  the system that is currently trying to repair itself."
\par Neville said slowly, "The error correction."
\par "Yes," said Weinstein, and looked at him with real approval. "Yes, the boy has it. Your Dr Fainrose is quite right that the country can be recovered from its correlations rather than from a copy. But a correlation is a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 statement about what agrees with what}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
. If you go through the population asking leading questions about what they remember, and each answer becomes true as it is given, then the agreements will be manufactured, and the syndrome will be clean, and the code will report itself intact, and it wil
l be intact \emdash  around nothing."
\par Beatrix said, "It would look like a successful recovery."
\par "It would }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 be}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306  
a successful recovery," said Weinstein. "Of the wrong state. Certified. Permanently. With no residue for anyone to appeal to, and no residue for anyone to eat." He sat back. "I have been dining on your species' abandoned lives since before you had knees w
orth mentioning, and I have never once had to explain to a government that a thing can be irrecoverable. You have made me }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 pedagogical}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 . I resent it."
\par Mrs Chain, who had not sat down, said, "You want us to stop them."
\par "I want the ledger to survive. Yes."
\par "For your own reasons."
\par "Entirely for my own reasons," said Weinstein. "Mrs Chain, I am eleven thousand times your age and I have never once acted from virtue. But my reasons and yours are, this month, pointing in the same direction, and you are in no position to be choosy about
 your allies." He glanced at the boxes. "Also, I am the only one of you who has read Phase Two, because I was invited into it at approximately half past ten this morning by a Deputy Under-Secretary who filled in a form about his mother."
\par Fainrose said, "You said my paper was wrong in one place."
\par "I did."
\par "Where."
\par Halpert Weinstein smiled, and it was not a comfortable smile, and Neville understood that they had arrived at the thing the old man had actually come to say.
\par "You write," said Weinstein, "as though a measurement were an event. A thing that happens, at a time, and after which there is one answer." He shook his great white head, gently. "That is the working assumption of every laboratory on this planet and it ha
s served you admirably and it is not true. When your Mr Venn scanned Mrs Chain's mantelpiece, what became definite for Mr Venn did not become definite for the mantelpiece, or for the county, or for me. There is no single moment at which the universe decid
es. There is only a widening region of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 agreement}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
, spreading outward at the speed of gossip, and there is no fact of the matter about the parts it has not reached."
\par Neville felt the hair on his arms go up.
\par "Then when Vale froze Sub-District 6\emdash "
\par "He froze it }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 for the grid}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
," said Weinstein. "Not for the woman remembering her sister. She was inside. Different question. Different answer. She could feel the shape of the name, could she not? She told them so, and they marked it no action required." He rose from the chair with 
an old man's effort and an entirely different creature's economy. "There is no view from nowhere, Mr Wren. Your Ministry believes it is standing outside the room. It is not standing outside the room. There is no outside. And when it finally asks its great
 national question about what everybody remembers, it will not be reading the answer."
\par He went to the door, and paused, and looked back at the five of them with something that in a warmer species would have been affection.
\par "It will be }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 writing}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
 it," said Halpert Weinstein. "And it will be inside the sentence when it does. Good evening. Do not invite me anywhere without meaning it."
\par He left.
\par The lamps came back up by one degree.
\par Jago sat down heavily in the chair the old man had used and then, after a moment's reflection, stood up again and chose another.
\par Beatrix said, to nobody, "We have an ally who eats the thing we are trying to save."
\par "He eats the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 offcuts}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
 of the thing we are trying to save," said Fainrose. "Which is worse, because it means he is telling the truth about wanting the rest of it intact." She was staring at the cabinet where his reflection had not been. "And he is right about the paper."

\par Mrs Chain finally sat.
\par "He cast no shadow," she said, "and he did not lie to us once. I have met the opposite arrangement all week and I know which I find more frightening."
\par Neville opened the green notebook.
\par He wrote:
\par He cannot enter unless asked.\line Neither can the measurement.\line They have built a national programme of asking.
\par Then:
\par There is no moment when the universe decides.\line There is only agreement, spreading.\line No one is outside the room.
\par And at the bottom, after a long pause:
\par Phase Two does not read memory.\line It writes it, and signs it,\line and the signature is inside the fact.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14114306 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs36\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
Quantum mechanics
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14114306 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 This chapter teaches the }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 measurement problem}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306  proper, and the specific paradox that sharpens it: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 Wigner's friend}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 .
\par Everything up to now has treated measurement as something that simply happens \emdash 
 an event with a time and an outcome. That is the working posture of every physics laboratory, and it is exactly what Weinstein attacks. The difficulty is this: quantum mechani
cs gives two incompatible rules for how states change. Left alone, a system evolves smoothly and reversibly under unitary dynamics (Chapter Five). Measured, it appears to jump discontinuously to a single definite outcome (Chapter Four). Nothing in the the
ory says where the boundary between the two rules lies, or what physically qualifies as a "measurement." That is the measurement problem, and it is unresolved.
\par Wigner's friend makes the problem impossible to look away from. Suppose Wigner's friend, inside a sealed laboratory, measures a system and obtains a definite result. For the friend, there is now a fact. But Wigner, outside, has performed no measurement 
\emdash  so by the theory's own rules he must describe the friend }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 and}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306  the apparatus }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 and}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306  
the system as one entangled superposition, with no definite outcome at all. Both descriptions are internally consistent. Both are licensed by the theory. They disagree about whether something happened.
\par This is Weinstein's correction to Fainrose, and the chapter's central turn: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 there is no view from nowhere.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306  Outcomes are relative to the systems that have become correlated with them. A measurement is not a universal event but the spread of agreement outward through an environment \emdash 
 which is precisely what decoherence describes (Chapter Seven), and precisely what decoherence does not by itself finish, since it explains why interference becomes inaccessible without ever singling out one branch as the real one.
\par Two consequences the novel now owns:
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls24\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid10895145 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 The frozen district.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306  Vale's grid established facts for the grid. It does not follow that anything became definite for a person inside an arrested interval \emdash 
 hence a woman who can feel the shape of a name she cannot finish, filed as requiring no action.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
The Einstein objection, in a vampire's mouth.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
 Weinstein's resemblance is not decorative. Einstein's real complaint was never that quantum mechanics gave wrong predictions; it was that a theory in which facts are observer-relative and measurement is a primitive is an }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 incomplete}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306  
description of nature. Putting that objection in the mouth of something that feeds on collapse is the joke and the argument at once: he has the strongest possible motive for insisting that the discarded branches are real.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14114306 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 And the invitation clause is n
ot whimsy. A measurement cannot be made without a question being posed in some basis (Chapter Two). Nothing is learned from a system that is not interrogated. A creature that requires an invitation to cross a threshold is, in this book, simply obeying the
 same rule as the physics \emdash  which is why a government that industrialises asking has industrialised its own exposure.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14114306 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs27 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs27\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 
What the reader learns
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls25\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid10895145 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 Quantum theory has two rules for change and does not say when to switch.

\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}"Measurement" is undefined within the theory, which is a scandal rather than a detail.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Wigner's friend shows two observers can consistently disagree about whether an outcome exists.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid14114306\charrsid14114306 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Facts may be relative to observers; there is no external vantage from which the universe settles accounts.
