of it. Dr Fainrose quoted four of your lines to me on the telephone this morning, in order to complain about them, which is the highest form of French compliment and, I understand, quite common in English also." He looked at the wall. "May I sit? It is a 
long time since I have sat on a wall and I should like to know whether it is still possible."
\par They sat on the wall.
\par "You are not with the Ministry," said Neville.
\par "Certainly not."
\par "Are you with him? The old man. Weinstein."
