\par "I have known Halpert Weinstein," he said, "for ninety-one years, and I have never once forgiven him, and I would trust him with my life and nothing smaller. He telephoned me last night. He said: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid13179129\charrsid13179129 Louis, they have a clerk who has understood four things by himself, and he is about to stop.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid13179129\charrsid13179129 
" He turned his head. "He was correct?"
\par Neville looked at the car park.
\par "I was a physicist," he said. "For about eleven minutes, in 1983. Then I was in electronics for forty years, and then I was a Transit Reconciliation Officer, and three days ago I started noticing things in a notebook, and every single thing I have noticed
 turns out to be a standard result with somebody's name on it." He heard how it sounded and did not much care. "Fainrose is doing stabiliser codes upstairs. Weinstein has been alive since before agriculture. And I write down }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid13179129\charrsid13179129 the house is larger than the form}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid13179129\charrsid13179129 
 and feel clever for eight minutes until somebody tells me it's called state reduction and was settled before I was born."
\par De Br\'e9vanne listened to all of this with grave attention, as though it were a technical report.
\par Then he said, "May I tell you what I was, in 1911."
\par "All right."
\par "I was twenty-two years of age. I had taken my degree in history." He said it without emphasis. "Medieval history. My family had been soldiers and diplomats for six hundred years and I was, by inclination, an archivist. I intended to spend my life on char
ters and legal instruments of the fourteenth century. My brother was the physicist. I was the one who read."
\par Neville turned to look at him.
\par "And then in 1911 my brother served as secretary to a congress of physicists," said de Br\'e9
vanne, "and brought home the papers, and I read them because I read everything, and I did not understand them, and I could not stop." He turned the hat over in his g
loved hands. "You must understand my position. I had no laboratory. I had no mathematics beyond the ordinary. I had no standing whatever. What I had was the habit of a historian, which is this: when two authorities disagree, one does not choose. One looks
