\par "And what were the two authorities."
\par "Light," said de Br\'e9
vanne, "which everybody knew was a wave, and which Einstein had shown behaved in some circumstances as though it were a great number of small hard things." He shrugged, very slightly. "The physicists were embarrassed by this. They spok
e of it as a difficulty to be managed. I was not embarrassed, because I was not a physicist, and so I did the thing that only an ignorant man would do, which was to take it seriously in both directions at once."
\par "Meaning."
\par "Meaning: everyone was asking how a wave could be a particle." He looked at Neville. "Nobody was asking whether a particle might be a wave. It is the same question turned round, Mr Wren, and turning a question round is not a discovery, it is a }{
