." He let that sit. "There are two ways to hold a piece of physics. One may receive it, in which case one possesses a fact and may repeat it. Or one may arrive at it, in which case one possesses the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid13179129\charrsid13179129 reason}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid13179129\charrsid13179129 
, and may go on from there to the next thing, which has no name yet. The second is the only kind that is any use in a crisis, and it is the only kind you have, and you are sitting on a wall in a car park despising yourself for it."
\par Neville found that his throat had gone rather tight, which was inconvenient.
\par "Fainrose does both," he said.
\par "Dr Fainrose is formidable and will save perhaps nine thousand houses this week," said de Br\'e9vanne. "But she cannot hear the question she was trained out of hearing. That is not a criticism. It is what training is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid13179129\charrsid13179129 for}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid13179129\charrsid13179129 
. It is expensive and it is necessary and it costs precisely one thing." He picked the hat up again. "Now. Weinstein told you last night that there is no view from nowhere, and that nothing is definite until agreement has spread. It frightened you."

\par "Yes."
\par "Good. It should. It is also not the only available account, and he did not tell you that, because he is an old snob who enjoys the vertigo." De Br\'e9
