\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 Bell's theorem}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 , and stages it against the two positions it was designed to discipline.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 Heitmann's position \emdash  operationalism.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  
The Copenhagen-flavoured view holds that the quantum formalism is an instrument for relating experimental preparations to experimental outcomes, and that questions about what a system possesses between measurements are not merely unanswerable but improper
ly formed. Its strength is discipline: it never overreaches, and it never gets caught. Its weakness is that it entrenches "measurement" as an unanalysed primitive, which is precisely the word Bell spent his career attacking as "unspeakable" \emdash 
 since any apparatus is itself made of atoms and governed by the same equations, no physical criterion marks off measurements from ordinary interactions.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 Tegn\'e9r's position \emdash  the mathematical universe and many worlds.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  If the Schr\'f6
dinger evolution is never interrupted, then measurement produces not one outcome but a branching structure in which all outcomes are realised, and the appearance of a single result comes from the observer being localised in one branch (Chapter
 Seven's decoherence supplies the branching mechanism). Its strength is economy: no collapse postulate, no special role for observers. The chapter's use of it is the political one, and it is not a straw man \emdash 
 a picture in which nothing is ever destroyed is 
genuinely available for misuse by anyone who needs harm to be relocated rather than caused, which is why Mrs Chain's objection matters. Whatever is true globally, the loss is suffered locally, by whoever is left holding it.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 Bell's theorem itself.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  Bell asked what follows if two assumptions hold jointly: }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 realism}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  (systems have definite properties independent of measurement) and }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 locality}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  (no influence propagates faster than light). From these alone \emdash  with no commitment to quantum mechanics at all 
\emdash  he derived an upper bound on the strength of correlations between measurements on separated systems. The CHSH form of the bound is
