\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid2584713 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 
They went into Sub-District 6 on the Thursday with two vans, a portable timing rack, four hired photodetectors, and a great deal of paperwork Beatrix Sloan had signed in a hand that dared anyone to query it.
\par The measurement would take two days to set up. Fainrose was in the primary school hall running cable with the concentration of a woman defusing something. Jago Flint had gone to acquire a fifth detector on terms nobody had asked about. And Neville Wren, w
ho had been sent out to find somewhere to work because he kept asking useful questions at inconvenient moments, had ended up in the reading room of the branch library on Coldharrow Street, at a table by the window, with the green notebook and eleven photo
copied pages from box seven.
\par There was one other person in the reading room.
\par He had been there when Neville arrived and gave every impression of having been there for some years: a slight man in his seventies, entirely ba
ld, in a plain shirt and unremarkable trousers, surrounded by an arrangement of papers so extensive and so orderly that it had colonised two additional tables without anybody apparently minding. He wrote in pencil. He wrote continuously. Every twenty minu
tes or so he stopped, looked out of the window at the car park of a shut-down carpet showroom, and then wrote some more.
\par At about four o'clock he said, without turning round, in a voice so quiet that Neville was not initially certain it had been aimed at him:
\par "You are the person doing the Bell test on the housing lattice."
\par Neville put down his pen. "How do you know that."
\par "Dr Bellew mentioned it." The man turned. He had a pleasant, unemphatic, slightly abstracted face and the mildest possible manner, and he spoke so softly that Neville found himself leaning forward before the sentence was finished. "I think it is an excell
ent idea. It is the correct instinct \emdash  to find the one claim that does not depend on taste. Forgive me. Leonhard von Wittenberg."
\par "Neville Wren."
\par "Yes." Von Wittenberg gathered three sheets of paper together, apparently at random, and came and sat down opposite him. "May I ask you something before you ask me anything?"
\par "Of course."
\par "When you say the country is held together by correlations" \emdash  he said it gently, as though it might be fragile \emdash  "what do you imagine the correlations are }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 
between}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 ?"
\par Neville thought about it.
\par "Houses," he said. "Records. Routes. Districts. People. The nodes on Fainrose's lattice."
\par "Yes. And what are those made of."
\par "Bricks. Paper. Copper. People are made of cells." He heard where this was going. "Cells are made of molecules, molecules of atoms, atoms of nuclei and electrons, nuclei of quarks."
\par "And quarks?"
\par "Are made of nothing. They're fundamental. Points."
\par Von Wittenberg smiled very slightly and looked out at the carpet showroom.
\par "That is the standard answer and it is the best-tested answer in the history of thought," he said. "It is also, I believe, wrong, and I have believed it for about fifty years and been unable to prove it, which is a condition your Duc de Br\'e9
vanne has described to you rather well, I understand."
\par Neville sat back.
\par "You're going to tell me they're strings."
\par "I am going to tell you something a little worse than that," said Leonhard von Wittenberg apologetically. "May I use your notebook? I shall use a clean page."
\par He drew, very small, a dot.
\par "A point particle," he said. "It has no size and no internal structure and no way of being anything other than what it is. If you wish to explain why there are so many different particles, you must simply }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 list}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  them, and we have listed them, and the list is seventeen items long and nobody knows why it is that list rather than another."

\par Beneath the dot he drew a tiny closed loop.
\par "Now suppose instead that the fundamental object is not a point but a small loop, of a length so short that every experiment ever performed sees it as a point. A loop can do something a point cannot." He looked up. "It can }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 vibrate}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 ."
\par "Different vibrations, different particles," said Neville.
\par "Different vibrations, different particles. Not seventeen unrelated facts. One object, seventeen notes." He said it without any drama whatever, which somehow made it land harder. "A violin string is not seventeen instruments. It is one string, played diff
erently."
\par Neville looked at the loop for a while.
\par "And the electron and the quark and the photon\emdash "
\par "Notes."
\par "And gravity?"
\par "Ah," said von Wittenberg, and for the first time something in his face brightened. "That is the part that persuaded me. You must understand \emdash 
 for fifty years the great scandal of physics was that our theory of the very small and our theory of gravity would not sit in the same room. Put them together and the answers came out infinite. Not large. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 Infinite}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 
, which is nature's way of saying that the question was malformed. Every attempt failed." He tapped the little loop. "But if the objects are loops rather than points, the infinities do not arise. And more than that \emdash 
 among the notes the loop can play, one of them, unavoidably, is a massless spin-two particle."
\par "Which is?"
\par "The graviton," said von Wittenberg. "You do not put gravity in. You cannot keep it out. It is simply one of the things the string does." He paused. "I have never got over that. I am seventy-four and I have not got over it."
\par Neville realised he had stopped taking notes and started again.
\par "Where's the catch."
\par "There are three, and I shall not hide them." Von Wittenberg counted them off on his fingers, mildly. "First: the strings are so small that no accelerator we could build on this planet or in this solar system could see one directly, and so the theory has 
never been tested. Not once. Dr Fainrose is entirely right to say so and she says it to me every time we meet."
\par "Second?"
\par "The mathematics does not work in four dimensions. It requires ten." He said this the way another man might mention that a recipe requires buttermilk. "Nine of space, one of time. And since we plainly experience three of space, the other six must be curle
d up small \emdash  at every point in what you think of as empty room there are six additional directions, wound so tightly that nothing you or I can do will ever push anything along them."
\par Neville looked out of the window at the carpet showroom.
\par "That is a great deal to ask of a car park," he said.
\par "It is, yes." Von Wittenberg looked pleased. "And the third catch is the one that mattered most, and it is the reason I am in a library in Sub-District 6 rather than at home. For a long time there were five different string theories. All consistent. All b
eautiful. All slightly different. Which was, you understand, an embarrassment \emdash  one had set out to explain why there is only one world and had produced five candidates and a great deal of silence."
\par "So what happened."
\par "In 1995," said Leonhard von Wittenberg, "I gave a talk."
\par He said it so quietly and so flatly that Neville nearly missed the size of it.
\par "The five theories were not five theories," said von Wittenberg. "They were five }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 descriptions}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 
. Each one was a different limited view of a single underlying structure \emdash 
 and one could pass between them by a set of relations we call dualities. Take one theory and turn a certain quantity up very high, and it becomes, exactly and provably, a different
 theory with that quantity turned very low. They are the same thing seen from different sides. And when one assembled all five together properly, they did not fit in ten dimensions. They fitted in eleven, and the fundamental objects were not only strings 
but also }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 membranes}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  \emdash  surfaces, sheets, extended things of various dimensions."
\par "And that's the theory."
\par "That is the theory, and it has no proper formulation yet, and so it has no proper name. I called it M-theory."
\par "What does the M stand for."
\par "Membrane," said von Wittenberg. "Or mystery. Or magic. According to taste. I have never settled it, and I have come to think the vagueness was accidentally honest."
\par Neville put his pen down.
\par "Professor von Wittenberg," he said. "I have to say this. In three days I have been told by an extremely old vampire that nothing is definite until agreement spreads. I have been told by a French duke that everything is definite and steered by a wave over
