nsional membranes that nobody can see and no experiment can reach." He looked at the little loop on the page. "I have a woman in this district who was stopped in the middle of remembering her sister's name. What am I supposed to }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 do}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  with strings?"
\par Von Wittenberg did not take offence. He seemed, if anything, to have been waiting for it.
\par "That is the right question and I shall answer it in the correct order," he said. "First: nothing. You are supposed to do nothing with strings. The Bell measurement is what matters this week and I would not have you divert a single cable from it. Anyone w
ho tells you that eleven dimensions will help you get an injunction is lying to you."
\par "All right."
\par "Second: I did not come here to tell you what everything is made of. Dr Bellew would rightly say that I cannot demonstrate it, and I cannot." He shifted the three sheets of paper he had brought and turned one round. "I came because of your black hole."

\par Neville looked down at the sheet.
\par It was covered in an extremely dense and entirely orderly hand, and among the symbols he could make out almost nothing, but at the top, underlined once, was written:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 4C \emdash  HORIZON ENTROPY \emdash  WHERE IS THE LEDGER}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 
\par His mouth went dry.
\par "Weinstein," he said.
\par "Halpert telephoned me on Tuesday and shouted at me for eleven minutes, which at his age is a considerable investment." Von Wittenberg's mouth twitched. "His concern, as I understand it, is that your Ministry has used a small engineered black hole as a di
sposal channel for suppressed alternatives, and that the object is now evaporating, and that when it has gone there will be no record of what was destroyed. He calls this the falsification of the ledger. He is frightened. I have known him ninety years and
 I have not heard him frightened before."
\par "And you can tell him it's all right?"
\par "No," said von Wittenberg. "But I can tell you why he may be wrong, and it is the one thing my subject has actually achieved."
\par He turned the sheet over.
\par "A black hole has an entropy," he said. "Bekenstein saw it and Hawking made it precise: the entropy is proportional to the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 area}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  
of the horizon. Not to the volume inside. To the area of the surface." He let that sit for a moment. "That was extremely strange, and for twenty years nobody could say what it was counting. Entropy counts states. Which states? Of what? The black hole was 
supposed to have almost no properties at all."
\par "And?"
\par "In 1996 two colleagues took a particular class of black hole in string theory, built it out of membranes and strings, counted the possible configurations of those objects \emdash  an ordinary counting problem, tedious but honest \emdash 
 and found that the number of states gave exactly the Bekenstein\endash 
Hawking entropy. Exactly. With the correct factor. No adjustment." He said it very quietly indeed. "That is the only occasion on which anyone has ever counted the microscopic states of a black hole and got the right answer,
 and it came out of strings, and I do not think it was luck."
\par Neville said slowly, "So there }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 are}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  states. It isn't featureless."
\par "It is not featureless. It has an enormous number of internal configurations, and the horizon area measures how many."
\par "Then when it evaporates\emdash "
\par "Then the question becomes whether the radiation carries them out," said von Wittenberg, "and here is the deeper thing, and it is why I asked you what the correlations were between." He turned the sheet again. "The area law suggests something outrageous, 
which we now half understand. It suggests that everything happening inside a region can be encoded on its }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 boundary}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
