\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 string theory and M-theory}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 , and then puts them to work on the black hole.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 Strings.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 
 The core proposal replaces zero-dimensional point particles with one-dimensional extended objects \emdash  strings \emdash  of characteristic length near the Planck scale, roughly }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 10^\{-35\}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  
metres. The payoff is twofold. First, the spectrum of vibrational modes of a single string reproduces a whole family of particle types, so the seventeen apparently unrelated fields of the Standard Model become notes on one instrument rather than seventeen
 brute facts. Second, and historically decisive: quantising gravity as a point-particle field theory produces non-renormalisable infinities, whereas the extended nature of the string smears interactions and removes them \emdash 
 and among the string's unavoidable massless modes is a spin-2 particle with exactly the properties of the graviton. Gravity is not added to string theory; it cannot be excluded from it.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 Extra dimensions.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 
 Consistency (anomaly cancellation) requires ten spacetime dimensions for superstring theory. The six unobserved spatial dimensions are taken to be compactified at microscopic scale, and the geometry of that compactification determines the observed partic
le content \emdash  which is also the source of the theory's most serious weakness, since there are enormous numbers of possible compactifications and no known principle selecting ours.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 M-theory and duality.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  
By the early 1990s there were five mutually distinct consistent superstring theories, plus eleven-dimensional supergravity \emdash 
 an embarrassment of riches. The resolution, presented by Witten at Strings '95, was that these are not competing theories but different limits of one underlying eleven-dimensional structure, related by }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 dualities}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 
: exact equivalences under which a strongly coupled description in one theory maps to a weakly coupled description in another. Its fundamental objects include not only strings but higher-dimensional }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713 branes}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid2584713\charrsid2584713  (membranes). It still lacks a complete formulation, and the "M" was deliberately left unglossed \emdash 
 membrane, mystery, or magic, according to taste.
\par The philosophical point the chapter borrows from duality is worth flagging, because it answers Chapter Thirteen: two descriptions can be exactly equivalent without either being the true one. Bellew's advice to hold several accounts at once stops looking l
ike indecision.
