The injunction was granted on the Tuesday by a judge who understood none of the physics and all of the paperwork, which turned out to be sufficient.
\par Beatrix Sloan had not argued that reality had been damaged. She had learned, by then, that courts do not adjudicate ontology. She had argued instead that the Ministry had certified nine thousand separate local determinations while holding, in its own fili
ng system, a measurement establishing that they were one act \emdash 
 and that a body which certifies nine thousand times what it knows to be one thing has not made an error of physics but a false instrument in a live system. Which is a thing the law has known how to dislike since the fourteenth century, as the Duc de Br
\'e9vanne observed with some satisfaction from the public gallery.
\par They got eleven weeks. It was not enough and nobody had expected enough.
\par Von Wittenberg's people listened to the horizon for nine of them, from a rented industrial unit outside Sub-District 6, with equipment that cost more than the district's entire housing stock and that Fainrose described, in her only published remark on the
 matter, as "an ear held against a burning wall."
\par Of the four hundred and eleven P-9 states, they recovered three hundred and eight.
\par It was not a triumph and Fainrose refused, in every interview afterwards, to let it be described as one. Three hundred and eight pending human states were unscrambled from the radiation and returned to the districts they had come out of: a child's route t
o school, a marriage in the middle of being decided, an apprenticeship, two reconciliations, a great many small unfinished intentions of no civic significance whatever. One hundred and three were not recovered, and their names were published, because Beat
rix insisted, and because Miss Dorothy Kell wrote to the inquiry to say that a list of the unrecovered was the only memorial such things could have.
\par They cut the coupling on the fourteenth of November at 06:04, which was Jago Flint's idea and which nobody objected to.
\par S across the paired dwellings of Sub-District 6 came up to 2.79 within forty minutes.
\par The districts resolved over the following month, badly and thinly and in the wrong order, and it looked on every screen exactly like collapse, and Crispin Vale \emdash  who by then had testified for eleven days and had stopped bringing biscuits \emdash 
 was blamed for the noise he had permitted rather than credited for the paralysis he had ended, precisely as Fainrose had told him he would be.
\par Mrs Elspeth Chain's sister was settled record. She was in the seventy-eight per cent. She did not come back.
\par But the photograph was on the mantelpiece at Twenty-Two Coldharrow Rise the following spring \emdash  the one of the two of them laughing at some unseen mishap, restored by a man pulling a cable out of a skirting board without permission \emdash 
 and Mrs Chain took the view, expressed at the inquiry and quoted rather widely afterwards, that a photograph a burglar had returned was worth more than a certificate a government had signed.
\par Halpert Weinstein left in December, without ceremony, having eaten nothing for six weeks.
\par "They have stopped asking on an industrial scale," he said, on the annex steps, with his coat over his arm and no shadow on the concrete. "It is a famine and I have brought it on myself and I find I do not mind as much as I expected. There will be other c
enturies." He shook Neville's hand with his cold one. "Mr Wren. You will want to know whether I was right or whether the Duc was, and I shall tell you honestly that I have had eleven thousand years and I do not know. Ask the fellow with the box. He has be
en waiting since 1935 and nobody has had the courtesy."
\par Neville said, "Which fellow with the box."
\par But Weinstein had already gone, in the manner of a thing that had not, strictly, been present.
\par Max Tegn\'e9r testified for the prosecution, at his own request, on the specific question of whether a multiverse makes cruelty free. His answer ran to four hours and can be fairly summarised as }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 no}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 .
\par Werner Heitmann sent a letter. It was two lines long. It said: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
The measurement was correctly performed and the inference is sound. I have never in my life been able to say more than that and I have never been able to say less.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
 Fainrose kept it in the front of the syndrome file for the rest of her career.
\par Jack Bellew did not appear again, which Neville chose to interpret as approval.
\par In February, Stephanie Fainrose was appointed to chair the reconstituted oversight body, on terms she dictated in writing and which included the phrase }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
no certificate may require the simultaneous determination of conjugate quantities}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
, and she offered Neville Wren the post of Principal Officer, Relational Integrity, which was a job title she had invented on a Thursday and defended for eight months.
\par He asked what the work was.
\par "Noticing," said Fainrose. "Professionally. With a budget."
\par "I'm not qualified."
\par "Neville," she said, "we have been over this in a car park."
\par He took it, and kept the green notebook, which by then had four hundred pages and had begun a second volume, and which is quoted in the schedule to the Act.
\par And on a Friday in March, in the reading room of the branch library on Coldharrow Street, at the table by the window overlooking the shut-down carpet showroom, he found a very old Austrian gentleman waiting for him with a wooden box on the table and two c
ups of coffee already going cold.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16342456 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs36\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
Prologue
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16342456 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
"Sit," said the old man. "I have ordered you a coffee and it is already unpleasant. Erwin Kastner. You have been sent to me by a dead Irishman, a French duke, and a vampire, which is a referral pattern I have not had since 1953."
\par He was very old and very Viennese, in a bow tie that had been tied by somebody who cared, with a great deal of white hair and an air of enormous and slightly disreputable charm. There was a wooden box on the table between them, about the size of a hatbox,
 with air holes.
\par Neville looked at the box.
\par "Don't," said Kastner. "It is empty. It has always been empty. It was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 always a joke}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
, Mr Wren, that is the thing nobody will accept \emdash  I invented it to be }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 ridiculous}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
, to show my colleagues that their position led somewhere absurd, and within twenty years it was on the side of coffee mugs. You cannot win. You make one sarcastic remark about a cat and it outlives your equation."
\par "Then why bring it."
\par "Because people sit down faster." Kastner pushed it aside. "Now. You have written four hundred pages and you have finished a thing, and you have come to a library on a Friday because you want somebody to tell you what it was all }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 for}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
, and Fainrose cannot tell you because she is a working physicist and von Wittenberg cannot tell you because he will start on the eleven dimensions. So it falls to me, who am not respectable."
\par "Weinstein said you'd been waiting since 1935."
\par "For someone to ask what the cat was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 about}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
, yes. Nobody asks. They ask whether it is alive." Kastner drank some of the terrible coffee. "Very well. I shall give you what you came for, and then you will go home. And I shall call it a Prologue, and you will object, and I shall explain."
\par He put down the cup.
\par "Here is the outlook, since you want the outlook.
\par "Your Ministry believed it was managing a country. It was in fact interfering, in a small and provincial way, with something that is not finished. And I do not mean the paperwork is not finished. I mean the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 universe}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
 is not finished, and it is not nearly finished, and everything you have lived through this year took place in what future arrangements of matter will regard as the opening seconds.
\par "The stars are the first thing to understand. There are stars now. This is not the normal condition. Star formation peaked long ago and is declining, and the gas will run down, and in something of the order of a hundred trillion years the last star will f
orm and the sky will begin to go out \emdash 
 not dramatically, one by one, the small red ones outliving all the rest with the patience of the parsimonious. And then there will be a very long time in which the galaxy is a place of embers: white dwarfs cooling, neu
tron stars cooling, brown dwarfs occasionally colliding and briefly lighting. That era is longer, Mr Wren, than the era of starlight by a factor you cannot usefully imagine. Almost all of history is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 afterwards}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 .
\par "Then, if the protons are not eternal \emdash  and we do not know, we have looked very hard and seen nothing, which only pushes the number out \emdash  then even the embers dissolve, and matter itself is a phase that ends.
\par "And then it is the age of the black holes, and they are the last structures, and they do exactly what your 4C did in a car park in Surrey: they evaporate. Slowly. Then less slowly. Then, at the very end, in a final bright indignity. The largest will take
 on the order of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 10^\{100\}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  
years, which is not a length of time so much as an insult to the concept. But they go. Everything that ever consolidated, goes.
\par "After that: a very thin, very cold, very dark space with a little radiation in it, expanding, and nothing in it that could be called an event.
\par "That is the outlook. You asked for it. That is what is next in the galaxy and in the universe, on the current evidence, and I have not exaggerated in either direction."
\par He let it sit for a moment, in the reading room, over the cold coffee.
\par "Now," said Erwin Kastner. "The interesting part, which is why I did not simply post it to you.
\par "Every physicist my age was taught that the universe runs down. Order becomes disorder; the coffee goes cold; the sun exhausts itself. Perfectly true. And when I was a younger man I looked at a living thing and thought: how does }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 that}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
 happen. Because a living thing does the opposite. It maintains itself. It holds a pattern against the current for eighty years.
\par "And the answer is not that life defies the law. The answer is that a living thing survives by }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 feeding on order}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  \emdash 
 by taking in structure and excreting disorder, by paying the universe's bill locally and passing the cost outward. It does not violate the accounts. It runs a very good scheme within them. A living thing is a place where order is temporarily concentrat
ed because it has arranged to be paid for elsewhere.
\par "You see it, of course. You have spent a year on it.
\par "Your Ministry, Mr Wren, was doing the same thing, badly, and without knowing it. It wished a district to be simple \emdash 
 that is, low in complexity, low in ambiguity, cheap to describe. And it discovered what every living system discovers, which is that you cannot simply }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 have}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  
that. You must pay for it, and the payment is that the disorder goes somewhere else. So they built a hole and put it in there. That was not a bureaucratic peculiarity. That was thermodynamics, conducted by people who had not been told they were doing ther
modynamics, and the reason it ended in a fire is that they never asked where the bill was going.
\par "Every institution that promises to make things simple is proposing to move complexity somewhere you cannot see. Every single one. Not sometimes. Always, and by law. That is the sentence I should like you to take away, and you may put it in your notebook,
 and you need not attribute it to me because Boltzmann said it first and less politely."
\par Neville was writing.
\par "And so \emdash  the outlook," said Kastner. "Yes. In the long run: the embers, the holes, the dark. I shall not soften it and I dislike people who do.
\par "But consider what is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 between}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  
now and that. Consider the enormous middle. There is going to be an extremely long period in which there is still order left to be spent, and in which anything that can arrange to be paid for elsewhere may persist, and think, and ask questions. Life did n
ot have to happen. Having happened, it has a very long time to work with, and the sort of thing it may become in a hundred million years does not bear guessing at and would probably not recognise us as ancestors so much as weather.
\par "And here is what I think, and I am eighty-nine and entitled to think it.
\par "The universe does not contain many places where it is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 described}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
. Almost everywhere, things simply proceed. But wherever something has assembled itself into an arrangement that can hold a model of the rest \emdash  a bird, a mathematician, a green notebook \emdash 
 the universe acquires, locally and temporarily, an account of itself. That is not nothing. That may be the rarest thing in it. It is certainly rarer than gravity.
\par "Your Ministry was engaged in reducing the number of such accounts, and calling it efficiency, and the reason that was a crime and not merely a mistake is that the accounts are the scarce commodity and the simplicity was not. Anyone can have simplicity. Y
ou can have all the simplicity you want at the end of time, and it will be perfect, and there will be nobody in it.
\par "So: what is next in the universe. More of this, for a very long while, and then less, and then none. And in the meantime, an unreasonably interesting middle, in which the correct posture is neither optimism nor despair but }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 attention}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  \emdash  which is your job title, I understand, and which somebody has finally thought to fund."
\par He picked up the empty box and put it on his lap.
\par "Now. Why a Prologue.
\par "Because you want an ending, Mr Wren, and I will not give you one, and my reason is technical rather than sentimental." He tapped the lid. "The whole trouble with my cat was that everybody read it as a story about a box. It is not. It is a story about the
 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 lid}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
. The absurdity I was pointing at is the belief that there exists a moment at which the matter is settled \emdash  that reality performs a closing act, once, cleanly, and then the fact is a fact and may be filed.
\par "There is no such moment. Your friend Weinstein was right about that much and it is the only thing he and I agree on. There is only the widening business of things becoming correlated with other things, indefinitely, forever, at the speed of gossip, and a
 state that has not yet finished resolving is not a mystery awaiting a verdict. It is the ordinary condition of everything.
\par "Three hundred and eight of your recovered items were pending. Not memories. Things that had not finished happening. You gave them back to people, and they are still not finished, and they will go on not being finished, which is what it means to be alive 
rather than certified.
\par "So this is not the end of your book. It is the front matter of what those three hundred and eight are going to do. It is a Prologue, and it is at the back, and if that offends the printers they may take it up with the second law."
\par He stood, and tucked the box under his arm, and drained the appalling coffee with the enthusiasm of a man who had survived worse.
\par "One last thing, since you have been polite and have not once mentioned the cat's welfare.
\par "You will be tempted, in your new post, to find out which of them was right. De Br\'e9vanne and his wave, Weinstein and his agreement, Tegn\'e9
r and his branches, von Wittenberg and his strings. You will want the answer, because you spent forty years in electro
nics and you want to know what the circuit is." Kastner put on his hat. "You will not get it. Not from me, not this century, possibly not ever. And you must not let that stop you from noticing things, because the noticing is the part that turned out to ma
tter and the interpretation was never what saved the district.
\par "What saved the district was a clerk who wrote down that a lie required electricity.
\par "Keep the notebook, Mr Wren. It is the only apparatus that has never once been wrong about what it saw."
\par He went out through the reading room, past the local history shelves, and cast a completely ordinary shadow, and did not look back.
\par Neville sat by the window for a while with the cold coffee and the noise of Coldharrow Street coming through the glass \emdash  a district in the middle of resolving, badly and slowly and in the wrong order, and not finished.
\par Then he took out the notebook, and turned to a clean page, and wrote at the top:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 PROLOGUE}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
\par And underneath:
\par Order is not free. Simplicity is always paid for somewhere else,\line and any institution that will not say where is lying or ignorant, and it does not matter which.
\par The universe is mostly not yet.\line Almost everything is still pending.\line That is not a fault in the filing.
\par Then, last, in the ordinary handwriting of a man who had somewhere to be:
\par There is no lid.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 THE END}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 \line \emdash  which is to say, the beginning of the part that has not been written down.

\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16342456 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs36\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
Physics
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16342456 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
The closing material carries three things, all Schr\'f6dinger's own.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 The cat, correctly.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  Schr\'f6
dinger devised the cat in 1935, in correspondence with Einstein, as a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 reductio}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  \emdash 
 an attack on the idea that quantum indeterminacy could be treated as a matter of course while being scaled up to macroscopic objects. It was never intended as an illustration of how nature works, and its popular career as a mystical emblem would have ap
palled him. Kastner's insistence that it is a story about the lid rather than the box is the historically faithful reading: the target is the assumption that there exists a definite moment at which the state resolves \emdash 
 the same unexamined "measurement" primitive that Bell attacked in Chapter Thirteen and that Wigner's friend dismantles in Chapter Eleven.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 Negentropy and life.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  In }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 What Is Life?}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  (1944), Schr\'f6
dinger asked how living matter maintains high internal order in a universe governed by the second law of thermodynamics. His answer: an organism is an open system that "feeds on negative entropy," importing order from its surroundings and exp
orting disorder, so that local order is bought at the price of a larger global entropy increase. Life does not violate the second law; it is an exceptionally good local arrangement within it. (He also, in the same book, argued that the hereditary material
 must be an "aperiodic crystal" \emdash  a prediction that helped point Watson and Crick toward DNA.)
\par This is the thermodynamic reading of the entire novel, arriving last. The Simplification Initiative wanted low-complexity districts, which is a low-entropy demand, and low entropy cannot be produced without exporting entropy elsewhere. The overflow channe
l into 4C was not administrative eccentricity; it was the thermodynamic cost of the programme, incurred by people who had not been told they were incurring it. Kastner's generalisation \emdash 
 that any institution promising simplicity is proposing to relocate complexity out of sight \emdash  is a statement about entropy accounting, not a metaphor.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 The far future.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
 The outlook Kastner gives follows the standard framework of the physical eschatology literature, which divides cosmic history into eras:
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls29\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid10895145 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 Stelliferous era.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  Star formation peaked billions of years ago and is declining as gas is consumed. The last stars are expected to form in roughly }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 10^\{14\}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  years, with low-mass red dwarfs \emdash  the most frugal burners \emdash  outlasting everything brighter.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 Degenerate era.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  A very long period dominated by stellar remnants: cooling white dwarfs, neutron stars, brown dwarfs, with occasional collisions producing brief flares. Vastly longer than the era of starlight.

\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 Proton decay (uncertain).}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  If baryon number is not exactly conserved, ordinary matter itself dissolves on timescales beyond }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 10^\{34\}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  years. Experiments have observed no proton decay, which sets lower bounds rather than settling the question.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 Black hole era.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  Black holes become the last remaining structures \emdash  and then evaporate by Hawking radiation (Chapter Six), accelerating as they shrink, with the largest surviving to something of order }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 10^\{100\}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  years before a final burst.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 Dark era.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456  A cold, thin, expanding space containing dilute radiation and no structures capable of hosting events.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16342456 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid16342456\charrsid16342456 
The one honest caveat: this depends on the behaviour of dark energy, and on proton stability, and on physics beyond the Standard Model that is not settled. It is the current best account, not a certainty.
